const EXPLANATIONS = {
"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: DiGeorge syndrome presents with thymic hypoplasia and impaired immunity, conotruncal cardiac defects, hypocalcemia caused by parathyroid hypoplasia, and craniofacial abnormalities. Parathyroid hypoplasia can lead to severe hypocalcemia after birth, with symptoms of neuromuscular instability and, occasionally, seizures.</p><p>DiGeorge syndrome is a disorder caused by a microdeletion in the 22q11.2 chromosome, which results in abnormal development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. It presents early in childhood with thymic hypoplasia and impaired immunity, conotruncal cardiac defects (eg, truncus arteriosus), hypocalcemia caused by parathyroid hypoplasia, and craniofacial abnormalities, such as low set ears, hypertelorism, micrognathia, and cleft lip and palate. It is associated with developmental delay. Laboratory evaluation will show leukopenia and decreased calcium concentrations, while a chest x-ray may demonstrate the absence of a thymic shadow, as in this patient. Further evaluation typically includes an echocardiography and kidney ultrasonography. Treatment is supportive. This patient presenting with jitteriness in the setting of hypocalcemia and truncus arteriosus likely has DiGeorge syndrome, making hypoparathyroidism the most likely diagnosis. Severe hypocalcemia can also present with seizures, laryngospasm, bronchospasm, and emesis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Hyperparathyroidism (Choice A), when primary, presents with hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia, most commonly caused by a parathyroid adenoma, and less likely hyperplasia or carcinoma. Hypercalcemia presents with abdominal pain, nephrolithiasis, bone pain, and altered mental status. Secondary hyperparathyroidism presents with hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, but this usually occurs in the setting of chronic kidney disease.</p><p>Pseudohypoparathyroidism (Choice C) is caused by an impaired response of the kidney to parathyroid hormone, which results in hypocalcemia. It is commonly associated with short stature, developmental delay, and osteodystrophy as part of a genetic disorder referred to as Albright hereditary osteodystrophy. It is not associated with cardiac defects.</p><p>Renal proximal tubular dysfunction (Choice D) commonly causes a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis as a result of an inability to reabsorb sodium bicarbonate. It can be a symptom of Fanconi syndrome, which also presents with hypophosphatemic rickets and growth delay.</p><p>Vitamin D-deficient rickets (Choice E) presents with hypocalcemia and bone weakening that can lead to deformities such as genu varum. It is usually secondary to a prolonged deficiency of vitamin D and would not be expected to present at birth.</p><p>X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (Choice F) is caused by an inherited inability of the renal proximal tubule to reabsorb phosphate and presents similarly to vitamin D-deficient rickets. It would not be expected to cause such a severe decrease in blood calcium concentrations.</p>",
"2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Clostridium difficile infection is an opportunistic and often hospital-acquired infection that commonly occurs after the use of antibiotics as a result of the loss of native colonic microbiota that allows competition-free proliferation of C. difficile. Patients typically present with watery diarrhea and abdominal pain; however, the diarrhea can become bloody and result in fulminant symptoms such as toxic megacolon and sepsis if severe.</p><p>Clostridium difficile infection is an opportunistic and often hospital-acquired infection that commonly occurs after the use of antibiotics, classically ampicillin and clindamycin, as a result of the loss of native colonic microbiota. This derangement in native microbiota presents an opportunity for the competition-free proliferation of C. difficile, which produces toxins that result in dysfunction of the brush border and a subsequent secretory diarrhea. C. difficile infections are associated with the presence of pseudomembranes that appear as patchy regions of white mucosa on colonoscopy. The severity of C. difficile colitis can vary, ranging from watery diarrhea and abdominal pain without constitutional symptoms in mild cases to toxic megacolon, colonic ischemia, and severe sepsis in fulminant cases. Treatment includes oral or rectal vancomycin, intravenous or oral metronidazole, fidaxomicin, or colectomy depending on the severity of the patient's presentation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Salmonella enteritidis (Choice B) is a gram-negative rod that can cause enteritis, presenting with watery diarrhea, cramping, and abdominal pain. It does not occur secondary to the use of antibiotics. Exposure to Salmonella enteritidis commonly occurs through inadequately cooked eggs or chicken.</p><p>Shigella sonnei (Choice C) is a gram-negative rod that can cause bloody diarrhea with systemic symptoms such as fever and myalgias. Bloody stools caused by shigellosis are often more frankly bloody than those of C. difficile colitis and often contain mucus or pus. Shigellosis does not occur secondary to the use of antibiotics.</p><p>Staphylococcus aureus (Choice D) can cause enteritis, which presents with vomiting, diarrhea, and dehydration. Symptoms are typically self-limited and resolve over 1 to 2 days. It results as a consequence of eating contaminated food (food poisoning) and is generally caused by exotoxin production.</p><p>Yersinia enterocolitica (Choice E) is a gram-negative rod that causes bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain. It results as a consequence of eating contaminated food or water.</p>",
"3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Serous otitis media commonly manifests as a sequela of an upper respiratory tract infection with evidence of a middle ear effusion and the absence of middle ear inflammatory findings. Dysfunction of the eustachian tube is the underlying cause. Most cases are self-limited.</p><p>Serous otitis media is suggested by the presence of a middle ear effusion in the absence of signs of acute otitis media. Eustachian tube obstruction or dysfunction leads to the accumulation of serous fluid within the middle ear. Such dysfunction is common in the setting of an upper respiratory tract infection because of the inflammation of the nasal mucosa occluding the ostium of the eustachian tube. Fluid accumulation typically presents with hearing loss, ear tugging, or vertigo. Patients with serous otitis media lack findings of fever, tympanic membrane erythema, or retrotympanic pus, which are more commonly found in the presence of acute otitis media. Treatment is supportive, as the condition is usually self-limited.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and H.</b></p><p>Acoustic neuroma (Choice A) may present with chronic sensorineural deafness or vertigo. They are often small, located near the cerebellopontine angle, and are associated with neurofibromatosis. Fluid in the middle ear cavity would not be expected.</p><p>Acute otitis media (Choice B) classically occurs in children, often from viral or bacterial pathogens. It presents with ear pain, fever, and an erythematous tympanic membrane with retrotympanic pus or middle ear effusion.</p><p>Bell palsy (Choice C), also known as facial (nerve) palsy, may result from viral infection or inflammation of the nerve and presents with unilateral weakness of the facial muscles including those of the forehead.</p><p>Benign positional vertigo (Choice D) results from translocation of an otolith into the semicircular canals and presents with acute episodes of postural vertigo.</p><p>Cholesteatoma (Choice E) is the accumulation of keratin and keratinized squamous epithelium within the middle ear and presents with mild, moderate, or severe conductive hearing loss, and occasionally with ipsilateral pain if the lesion has invaded surrounding structures.</p><p>Mastoiditis (Choice F) describes bacterial infection of the mastoid air cells. It results from untreated or recurrent otitis media and presents with postauricular pain, swelling, and erythema.</p><p>Perforation of the tympanic membrane (Choice G) results in acute conductive hearing loss and would be visible on otoscopic examination.</p><p>Retraction pocket of the tympanic membrane (Choice H) results from chronic otitis media and may result in hearing loss if the architecture of the ossicles is disrupted. Acute serous effusion does not result directly from tympanic membrane retraction.</p>",
"4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Adolescents should receive immunizations against HPV and meningococcus, and a booster for tetanus, diphtheria, and pertussis.</p><p>The most appropriate vaccine series for this patient would include human papillomavirus (HPV), meningococcal, and tetanus-diphtheria-acellular pertussis (TdaP) vaccines. HPV is a nonenveloped, double-stranded DNA virus of the papillomaviridae family that causes genital warts. Infection with HPV is a significant and preventable risk factor for cervical, penile, anal, and oropharyngeal cancer. Administration of the HPV vaccine is recommended for both male and female patients between the ages of 9 and 26 years. For both sexes, it provides protection against genital warts, with the additional benefit of decreased cervical cancer risk in women. Meningococcal conjugate vaccine is indicated for all children, with an additional serogroup B vaccine indicated for all children over 10 years old at increased risk for meningococcal disease (eg, dormitory, military living). This would be appropriate in this patient who may attend college and participate in dormitory living. Although the patient received the diphtheria-tetanus toxoid (Td) at the age of 10 years, she will need the TdaP vaccine. Children receive a childhood series of immunization for diphtheria, pertussis, and tetanus (DTaP) involving pertussis antigens. The TdaP includes a tetanus toxoid, decreased diphtheria toxoid, and acellular pertussis and is recommended for adolescents starting at age 11 years.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Hepatitis B immunization (Choices A, C, and D) is typically administered to newborns or to adults who previously did not receive the vaccine. The patient's immunizations were up-to-date when she began kindergarten, meaning she likely received the hepatitis B vaccine as a newborn.</p><p>The Td booster (Choices A and F) is recommended every 10 years for adults who have previously received the TdaP vaccine.</p><p>Measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) (Choices C and F) is a routine vaccination that involves two doses: one at 12 to 15 months and one between 4 and 6 years. The patient's immunizations were up-to-date when she began kindergarten, meaning she likely received the MMR vaccine.</p><p>Pneumococcal conjugate, 7-valent (PCV-7), and inactivated poliovirus (IPV) (Choices D and E) would also not be indicated in this patient. PCV-7 was the first pneumococcal vaccine that provided protection against seven serotypes of pneumococcal bacteria. The current pneumococcal vaccine is PCV-13, which provides further protection with six other serotypes. It is typically given at 2, 4, 6, and 12 to 15 months of age. IPV is similarly given at 2, 4, 6 to 18 months old, and 4 through 6 years old. Given that the patient has received all vaccines prior to kindergarten, she likely already has had these immunizations.</p>",
"5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypothyroidism classically presents with a combination of fatigue, weight gain, constipation, and cold intolerance, and it may demonstrate heavy or irregular periods. Physical examination may show bradycardia, dry, edematous skin, and delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes. Hashimoto thyroiditis is a common cause of primary hypothyroidism, especially in patients with a history of autoimmune disease. Serum TSH concentration is generally increased, while concentrations of thyroid hormone are decreased.</p><p>Hypothyroidism classically presents with a combination of fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and may demonstrate heavy or irregular periods. Physical examination may show bradycardia, dry, edematous skin, and delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes. In this patient, her symptoms of tiredness, progressively heavier and irregular periods (menometrorrhagia), and bradycardia, in the setting of a preexisting autoimmune disease (type 1 diabetes mellitus) suggest the presence of a second autoimmune condition, likely acquired hypothyroidism secondary to Hashimoto thyroiditis. While many causes of hypothyroidism exist, Hashimoto disease is a common cause of painless thyroiditis, which may present with a palpable, enlarged thyroid gland, as in this patient. With progressive inflammation and destruction of the thyroid parenchyma, circulating concentrations of thyroxine and triiodothyronine decrease, with resulting feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary leading to increased concentrations of thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), and prolactin. The increased TSH concentration is measurable in serum and provides ongoing trophic stimulation to the thyroid gland. Increased prolactin may cause breast development, galactorrhea, and may promote irregular anovulatory cycles or amenorrhea through feedback on gonadotropin-releasing hormone. Treatment includes supplementation with levothyroxine and frequent follow-up visits to evaluate for therapeutic effectiveness, including monitoring of serum TSH concentration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Cortisol (Choice A) is increased in primary and secondary Cushing syndrome, often caused by a hyperfunctioning adrenal adenoma, hyperplasia, or an adrenocorticotropin-secreting tumor. Its concentrations are generally not affected, or may be only mildly deviated from normal limits, in the setting of hypothyroidism.</p><p>Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (Choice B) is an androgen produced by the adrenal zona reticularis. It may be increased in cases of polycystic ovarian syndrome, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, and Cushing disease, though is more often used to identify a source of hyperandrogenism in patients with virilization, precocious puberty, or infertility. When increased, the adrenal gland is likely the source of the endogenous increase in steroid synthesis, and when normal, the source is often a functional ovarian or testicular tumor.</p><p>Estrogen (Choice C) is a steroid produced by the theca cells of the ovary. It promotes bone development and regulates endometrial development during the menstrual cycle. This patient's irregular cycles are more attributable to the effects of hypothyroidism and prolactin than to deranged estrogen synthesis.</p><p>Luteinizing (Choice D) hormone is produced by the anterior pituitary gland and, along with follicle-stimulating hormone, controls the ovarian cycle. It increases sharply around the midpoint of the menstrual cycle, triggering ovulation. Its concentrations may be decreased in hypothyroidism as a result of the negative feedback from potentially increased prolactin.</p>",
"6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A PDA results from failure of the normal, extracardiac embryonic shunt between the pulmonary artery and aorta to appropriately close in the neonatal period. It results in an extracardiac left-to-right shunt and classically presents with a continuous machine-like murmur.</p><p>A patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) presents with a heart murmur described as a continuous \"machinery-like\" murmur best heard in the left second intercostal and left infraclavicular regions. Since blood flow through the PDA occurs in both systole and diastole, the sound will be present throughout the cardiac cycle. The ductus arteriosus is one of several physiologic shunts existing during the prenatal period. In utero, oxygenated blood is obtained from the placenta, not from the lungs, and the ductus arteriosus (DA) allows the shunting of oxygenated blood from the pulmonary artery directly to the aorta while bypassing the fetal lungs, which are filled with amniotic fluid. At birth, closure of the DA occurs by the release of prostaglandins and increased arterial oxygen tension. With increased blood oxygenation and decreased pulmonary vascular resistance following birth or with indomethacin use, prostaglandin production falls and the ductus closes. Certain congenital heart diseases require the presence of a shunt to allow arteriovenous admixture of oxygenated blood, for example, transposition of the great vessels. In the absence of such a requirement for shunting, a PDA results in a chronic extracardiac left-to-right shunt that may result in pulmonary hypertension or right-sided heart failure, with the risk for Eisenmenger syndrome if uncorrected. PDAs that are small often go undetected until adulthood because the left-to-right shunt is not significant enough to cause symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Extracardiac right-to-left shunt (Choice B) occurs in the setting of a chronic left-to-right shunt that results in increased pulmonary blood flow and progression of pulmonary hypertension with consequent Eisenmenger syndrome and reversal of the shunt. This may occur in the setting of a chronic, uncorrected PDA. An extracardiac right-to-left shunt can also occur in pulmonary arteriovenous malformations, which are a characteristic finding in patients with hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia, which typically presents with recurrent hemoptysis and an increased risk for paradoxical emboli.</p><p>Intracardiac atrial left-to-right shunt (Choice C) occurs with an atrial septal defect (ASD), which is a common congenital malformation of the interatrial septum. It classically presents with a fixed, split S2 as a result of increased blood flow across the pulmonic valve, and a low-grade physiologic ejection murmur on cardiac auscultation. Intracardiac atrial right-to-left shunt (Choice D) may result from a chronic, uncorrected ASD that progresses to Eisenmenger syndrome.</p><p>Intracardiac ventricular left-to-right shunt (Choice E) occurs with a ventricular septal defect (VSD), which is characterized by a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border. Intracardiac ventricular right-to-left shunt (Choice F) may occur with a chronic, uncorrected VSD that progresses to Eisenmenger syndrome.</p>",
"7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pseudotumor cerebri, or idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH), presents with headache, papilledema, and increased opening pressure on lumbar puncture in young, obese women without any underlying mass on neuroimaging. Hypervitaminosis A is associated with an increased risk for the development of IIH.</p><p>Pseudotumor cerebri, or idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH), is characterized by signs of increased intracranial pressure including headache, papilledema, and increased opening pressure on lumbar puncture without any underlying pathology to cause such findings on neuroimaging. It is primarily seen in young women who are overweight. Although there is no malignant cause underlying the increased intracranial pressure, it may cause debilitating headaches and permanent vision loss. Headaches that are secondary to increased intracranial pressure often are worse when lying down and relieved by standing. The most common risk factors for the development of pseudotumor cerebri include female sex and obesity. However, hypervitaminosis A from increased dietary intake is another potential cause of IIH. This can be from excessive dietary intake or from the use of retinoid medications such as isotretinoin or all-trans retinoic acid. Other medications associated with IIH include tetracyclines, danazol, and oral contraceptives.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is not associated with increased B-complex vitamins including B6 (pyridoxine) (Choice B). Because B-complex vitamins and vitamin C (Choice C) are water soluble and thus easily excreted in the urine, conditions caused by an excess of these vitamins are rare. Neither is associated with IIH.</p><p>Vitamin D (Choice D) and vitamin E (Choice E) are, like vitamin A, fat-soluble. Excessive amounts of these vitamins can develop from exogenous supplementation, which may sometimes be seen in fad dieters. Excessive vitamin D induces hypercalcemia and its manifestations such as confusion, polyuria, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, muscle weakness, and nephrolithiasis. Excessive vitamin E may alter the metabolism of vitamin K, enhancing the anticoagulant effects of warfarin in a patient taking this medication. Neither predisposes to the development of IIH.</p>",
"8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pyloric stenosis presents at several weeks of life and is characterized by repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds, leading to dehydration and a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Diagnosis is made with ultrasonography, and treatment is with surgical pyloromyotomy.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis is caused by hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter, leading to gastric outlet obstruction and vomiting. It commonly presents with nonbloody, nonbilious emesis after feedings, and begins around 3 to 6 weeks of age. It is more common in male infants. Symptoms may begin with occasional vomiting, then progress to projectile emesis after every feed, dehydration, and malnutrition as the stenosis worsens. Repeated bouts of vomiting can cause metabolic and electrolyte derangements, classically a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis caused by the loss of hydrochloric acid and volume contraction. On physical examination, an ovoid lump may be palpated in the upper abdomen, which correlates with the hypertrophied pylorus. Diagnosis of pyloric stenosis is by abdominal ultrasonography demonstrating thickened pyloric musculature. Treatment is with a surgical pyloromyotomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>X-ray of the abdomen (Choice A) may show distention of the stomach in pyloric stenosis and can also be used to evaluate other intra-abdominal pathology, such as bowel obstruction. However, it would not clearly outline hypertrophy of the pylorus.</p><p>X-ray of the chest (Choice B) is used in the evaluation of chest pain, shortness of breath, and cough to assess for intrathoracic pathology. Upright chest x-ray is also used in suspected cases of bowel perforation. It is not commonly used to evaluate recurrent emesis in absence of suspected complications, although it could demonstrate a hiatal or diaphragmatic hernia if present. It plays no role in the diagnosis of pyloric stenosis.</p><p>Barium enema (Choice C) is used to examine for abnormalities in the large intestine, such as colon cancer. It can also be used in children to evaluate for Hirschsprung disease and intestinal malrotation. It is not useful in the diagnosis of pyloric stenosis.</p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice E) would likely demonstrate the hypertrophic pyloric musculature. However, it is associated with a much greater radiation exposure than ultrasonography, making it a less appropriate next step in diagnosis.</p>",
"9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Laryngotracheobronchitis, also known as croup, typically presents with fever, rhinorrhea, a barking cough, and inspiratory stridor. It is commonly secondary to a viral infection from parainfluenza. Stridor refers to a high-pitched sound secondary to turbulent airflow from an underlying upper airway obstruction, often caused by inflammation (epiglottitis, laryngitis, tracheitis) or structural lesions (foreign body, laryngeal tumor).</p><p>Stridor refers to a high-pitched sound caused by turbulent airflow through obstructed or narrowed upper airway structures, such as the pharynx, larynx, or trachea. It can be caused by illnesses such as croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) or epiglottitis, or by structural lesions such as a laryngeal tumor, foreign body, tracheomalacia, or laryngospasm. This patient presenting with a new fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, and respiratory distress is likely suffering an acute infection involving the structures of the larynx. Croup is characterized by fever, rhinorrhea, a barking cough, and inspiratory stridor. Illness is typically mild and self-limited; however, inflammation of the upper airway can result in life-threatening airway obstruction. It can be caused by viral or bacterial infections, commonly parainfluenza virus. Plain x-rays may disclose subglottic narrowing of the airway.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Alveoli and interstitial tissues (Choice A) are inflamed in pneumonia. Pneumonia characteristically presents with cough, fever, and areas of opacification on chest x-rays. Pulmonary auscultation may disclose crackles, wheezes, or decreased breath sounds. Stridor is associated with inflammation of the upper airway.</p><p>Bronchi (Choice B) are inflamed in bronchitis, which is a common cause of cough in young children. Typical respiratory pathogens causing bronchitis are viruses, Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Moraxella catarrhalis. Stridor may affect portions of the bronchi, although it is centered in the upper airways of the larynx and trachea.</p><p>Bronchioles (Choice C) are inflamed in bronchiolitis, which is a common lower respiratory tract infection in children younger than 2 years of age, classically caused by respiratory syncytial virus. Auscultation may disclose wheezes and crackles.</p><p>Chest wall (Choice D) pathology may result in stridor in the setting of external compression of the airway from a chest wall mass or an enlarging abscess. Croup is a more likely diagnosis in this patient with acute symptoms.</p>",
"10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Iron deficiency anemia presents with hypochromic, microcytic erythrocytes on peripheral smear. Iron deficiency is prevalent in children as a result of insufficient dietary intake, especially in children primarily fed cow's milk without iron-fortified formula or food supplementation. It is treated with oral iron supplementation.</p><p>Iron deficiency anemia (IDA) presents with microcytic, hypochromic anemia with otherwise normal shape of red blood cells. IDA is extremely prevalent worldwide, especially in developing countries, in pregnant women, and in children. Infants and children are particularly vulnerable to IDA as a result of rapid growth and dietary deficiencies. The patient has been fed primarily cow's milk for the past 6 months, along with table food, while formula was stopped at 6 months of age. This places the infant at risk for iron deficiency given the insufficient amount of iron in cow's milk. Many types of formulas and foods are fortified with iron to ensure appropriate supplementation. Iron is required for the synthesis of heme, which is a necessary component of the hemoglobin molecule, and thus, of erythrocytes. It functions to shuttle oxygen to and from peripheral tissues. Erythrocytes on the peripheral blood smear are hypochromic as a result of deficient hemoglobin concentration. It is hypothesized that erythrocytes are microcytic as a result of continuing erythrocyte division in order to reach an adequate hemoglobin concentration. Because hemoglobin stores are inadequate, cell division continues beyond what would normally occur and causes the cells to be smaller than normal. Treatment in the case of poor dietary intake is with oral iron supplementation. If severe, parenteral iron can be administered.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Folic acid (Choice A) deficiency presents with macrocytic, megaloblastic anemia. Folic acid deficiency may be observed in infants with malabsorptive diseases, such as celiac disease, or who are fed goat's milk, which contains insufficient concentrations of folic acid.</p><p>Phosphatidylcholine (Choice C) is an important component of pulmonary surfactant. Deficiency presents with respiratory distress syndrome and is common in premature neonates. Phosphatidylcholine deficiency is not typically accompanied by anemia.</p><p>Vitamin B6 (Choice D), or pyridoxine, deficiency is uncommon in children and infants and may present with seizures, peripheral neuropathy, and sideroblastic anemia.</p><p>Vitamin E (Choice E) is an antioxidant that protects cells from free radical damage. Deficiency may present with hemolytic anemia and generalized muscle weakness. Vitamin E deficiency may occur in the neonatal period, especially in premature infants, but is less common in infancy.</p>",
"11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In infants, urinary tract infections may present solely with fever, irritability, or fussiness. Urinalysis often shows leukocytes, bacteria, and the presence of nitrites and leukocyte esterase. Urinary tract infections should be treated with antibiotics with sufficient coverage of gram-negative rods.</p><p>Urinary tract infections are characterized by dysuria, urinary urgency, frequency, or incontinence. In infants, urinary tract infections may present solely with fever, irritability, or fussiness. Urinalysis often shows leukocytes, bacteria, and the presence of nitrites and leukocyte esterase. Risk factors for urinary tract infections include being less than 1 year of age, female, or an uncircumcised male. It should also be suspected especially when there is no other source of infection identified, such as otitis media or upper respiratory tract infection. Urinary tract infections should be treated with antibiotics with sufficient coverage of gram-negative rods. Cefotaxime is a third-generation cephalosporin that would be sufficient to treat this patient's urinary tract infection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cefazolin (Choice A) is a first-generation cephalosporin. It does have some activity against E. coli but lacks activity against many gram-negative rods. First-generation cephalosporins may be appropriate for a urinary tract infection if culture and sensitivity data support a susceptible organism. A third-generation cephalosporin is a broader choice.</p><p>Nafcillin (Choice C) is used to treat gram-positive organisms, especially methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. It would not be the antibiotic of choice in a urinary tract infection, which is typically caused by gram-negative rods. Similarly, penicillin (Choice D) may be inadequate to treat this urinary tract infection as it has limited coverage of gram-negative bacteria.</p><p>Vancomycin (Choice E) inhibits bacterial cell wall peptidoglycan formation in gram-positive bacteria by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala. It is effective in the treatment of resistant organisms such as methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus and ampicillin-resistant Enterococcus species. It does not have significant activity against gram-negative pathogens.</p>",
"12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypertensive encephalopathy requires the careful lowering of blood pressure, which must be done quickly while avoiding excessively rapid drops in blood pressure. Sodium nitroprusside is a potent vasodilator and is a preferred agent in this scenario because of its short half-life, allowing for the careful titration via intravenous infusion.</p><p>Hypertensive encephalopathy may result from acute and severe increases in blood pressure. It may manifest with headache, vomiting, agitation, altered mental status, and seizure and may progress to obtundation and coma. Visual symptoms may result from cortical hypertension or from hypertensive retinopathy. Treatment includes carefully lowering the blood pressure while avoiding excessively rapid drops in blood pressure, which can cause cerebral or cardiac ischemia. In general, a decrease in the mean arterial pressure of 25% within the first hour is appropriate, with further gradual correction over the following day or more. Sodium nitroprusside is a potent vasodilator and is a preferred agent because of its very short half-life, allowing it to be titrated via intravenous infusion.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Diazoxide (Choice A) is also a potent vasodilator and is occasionally used for the treatment of hypertensive emergency. However, its longer half-life makes it less optimal in comparison to nitroprusside.</p><p>Furosemide (Choice B) is a loop diuretic. It is not typically used as a primary agent in the treatment of hypertensive emergency.</p><p>Hydralazine (Choice C) is an effective agent for lowering blood pressure. However, it has a long half-life and its efficacy is often unpredictable, which may lead to unintended precipitous drops in blood pressure.</p><p>Lorazepam (Choice D) is useful for the treatment of seizures, including status epilepticus. This patient's obtundation occurs, however, in the setting of extreme hypertension with potential cerebral edema, which is a more likely cause of his seizure than primary epilepsy. With correction of the blood pressure, further seizure activity should resolve.</p>",
"13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vascular tumors such as tufted hemangiomas and kaposiform hemangioendotheliomas can be complicated by Kasabach-Merritt syndrome, which refers to the sequestration and activation of platelets by the tumor, leading to thrombocytopenia and consumptive coagulopathy.</p><p>Vascular tumors such as tufted hemangiomas and kaposiform hemangioendothelioma are rare in infancy. Tufted hemangiomas present as firm, red lesions with ill-defined borders on the arms, legs, and trunk, often sparing the head and face. They become larger, darker, and indurated with age, although most stabilize in childhood. Kaposiform hemangioendothelioma is a subcutaneous vascular mass that appears as a purpuric raised lesion, most commonly on the extremities. It also tends to become darker and indurated with time. Both of these vascular tumors are associated with Kasabach-Merritt syndrome, which presents with bleeding, ecchymoses, and petechiae as a result of platelet sequestration by the tumor and an associated coagulopathy. Development of this syndrome typically presents with a rapid enlargement of the tumor, thrombocytopenia, hypofibrinogenemia, and increased D-dimer concentration. Treatment options include steroids, chemotherapy (eg, vincristine), and immunomodulators (eg, sirolimus).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (Choice A) presents with bleeding, thrombocytopenia, increased prothrombin and partial thromboplastin times, decreased plasma fibrinogen concentration, and increased D-dimer concentration. It can be caused by trauma, severe sepsis, and, occasionally, malignancy. It is less likely than Kasabach-Merritt syndrome in this otherwise healthy patient with a vascular tumor.</p><p>High-output congestive heart failure (Choice B) presents with shortness of breath, weight gain, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, and peripheral edema, often in the setting of an arteriovenous fistula, cirrhosis, sepsis, or severe anemia. It does not present with bruising and petechiae.</p><p>Impaired tissue growth due to altered vascular supply (Choice C) and increased tissue growth due to increased vascular supply (Choice D) are incorrect, as the altered vascular supply to the vascular tumor does not affect tissue growth. Rather, the altered vasculature sequesters and activates platelets, leading to thrombocytopenia and consumptive coagulopathy.</p>",
"14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Growing pains are a common cause of musculoskeletal pain and present with deep, bilateral, nonarticular pain in the evening or at night. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Treatment is symptomatic with massage, acetaminophen, and reassurance.</p><p>Growing pains are a common cause of musculoskeletal pain in children. The pain has characteristic features, including a bilateral, nonarticular presentation, commonly in the thighs or calves, and commonly occurs in the evening or at night. The cause of growing pains is unknown, as the pain does not typically occur at the sites of bone growth. Physical examination is normal and does not show any evidence of bone or joint inflammation or malformation. Patients do not usually have impairment of physical activity. The pain usually resolves by morning and treatment is symptomatic with massage, acetaminophen, and reassurance.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, L, M, N, O, and P.</b></p><p>Chondromalacia of the patella (Choice A) is a cartilaginous injury that may cause anterior knee pain. It most commonly occurs as an overuse injury in athletes.</p><p>Discitis (Choice B) is an infection of the intervertebral disc. It may present in children with back pain and refusal to walk.</p><p>Hypermobility syndrome (Choice D) presents with joint hypermobility and associated joint and muscle pain.</p><p>Iliotibial band syndrome (Choice E) is caused by friction between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral epicondyle. It occurs most commonly as an overuse injury in athletes and presents with lateral knee pain.</p><p>Ligamentous injury (Choice F) of the knee, such as injury to the anterior or posterior cruciate ligaments, typically occurs as an acute sports injury and presents with abrupt knee pain and swelling.</p><p>Osgood-Schlatter disease (Choice G) refers to osteochondrosis or traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle that typically occurs in adolescent, athletic children. It presents with anterior knee pain and swelling.</p><p>Osteoid osteoma (Choice H) are benign bony tumors that commonly occur in long bones and present with pain and refusal to bear weight. The pain would not likely be bilateral.</p><p>Osteomyelitis (Choice I) is an infection of bone and bone marrow, and typically presents acutely, subacutely, or chronically from direct inoculation or hematogenous spread. Patients typically complain of pain, difficulty bearing weight, and overlying erythema.</p><p>Patellofemoral compression (Choice J) is caused by tightness of the lateral retinaculum and presents with knee pain when climbing stairs or with prolonged sitting.</p><p>Referred pain from the hip (Choice K) may occur in the setting of hip injury or arthritis. Pain is typically unilateral and accompanied by limping and refusal to bear weight.</p><p>Reflex sympathetic dystrophy (Choice L) is a form of chronic nerve pain that presents with chronic burning pain of an extremity and with associated changes to the skin.</p><p>Rheumatoid arthritis (Choice M), juvenile rheumatoid arthritis in pediatric patients, occurs before the age of 16 and classically presents with joint pain and inflammation along with extra-articular findings such as uveitis, lymphadenopathy, and rash.</p><p>Septic arthritis (Choice N) is bacterial infection of the synovial space, most commonly by Staphylococcus aureus, and presents with acute onset joint pain, erythema, swelling, refusal to bear weight, and signs and symptoms of sepsis.</p><p>Subluxation of the patella (Choice O) refers to partial dislocation of the patella, commonly as an acute sports injury in young athletes. Patients present with acute knee pain.</p><p>Toxic synovitis (Choice P) is transient inflammation of the synovium that commonly occurs in pediatric patients less than 10 years old and is often preceded by an upper respiratory infection or viral prodrome. It presents with inability to bear weight.</p>",
"15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Septic arthritis is a common orthopedic emergency in the pediatric population. It presents with acute onset joint pain, erythema, swelling, refusal to bear weight, and signs and symptoms of sepsis.</p><p>Septic arthritis commonly occurs in children. Risk factors include trauma to the joint and procedures causing transient bacteremia (eg, dental procedures). Septic arthritis presents with systemic signs and symptoms of illness such as fever, chills, myalgias, arthralgias, and nausea, along with a painful, erythematous, swollen joint with limited range of motion, commonly the knee or hip. Patients may refuse to bear weight. It may occur concomitantly with osteomyelitis of adjacent bone. Serum laboratory values typically show leukocytosis, and increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein concentrations. Synovial fluid aspiration demonstrates a high concentration of white blood cells and may disclose bacteria, commonly Staphylococcus aureus. Treatment includes antibiotics and surgical washout of the affected joint.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, L, M, O, and P.</b></p><p>Chondromalacia of the patella (Choice A) is a cartilaginous injury that may cause anterior knee pain. It most commonly occurs as an overuse injury in athletes.</p><p>Discitis (Choice B) is an infection of the intervertebral disc. It may present in children with back pain and refusal to walk.</p><p>Growing pains (Choice C) are idiopathic and occur bilaterally at night in a nonarticular distribution. They are not accompanied by any abnormal examination findings.</p><p>Hypermobility syndrome (Choice D) presents with joint hypermobility and associated joint and muscle pain.</p><p>Iliotibial band syndrome (Choice E) is caused by friction between the iliotibial band and lateral femoral epicondyle. It occurs most commonly as an overuse injury in athletes and presents with lateral knee pain.</p><p>Ligamentous injury (Choice F) of the knee, such as injury to the anterior or posterior cruciate ligaments, typically occurs as an acute sports injury and presents with abrupt knee pain and swelling.</p><p>Osgood-Schlatter disease (Choice G) refers to osteochondrosis or traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle that typically occurs in adolescent, athletic children. It presents with anterior knee pain and swelling.</p><p>Osteoid osteoma (Choice H) are benign bony tumors that commonly occur in long bones and present with pain and refusal to bear weight. Patients would not present with fever or signs of sepsis.</p><p>Osteomyelitis (Choice I) is an infection of bone and bone marrow. Patients typically complain of pain, difficulty bearing weight, and overlying erythema. This patient's examination is more suggestive of an infection of the joint space, rather than of adjacent bone.</p><p>Patellofemoral compression (Choice J) is caused by tightness of the lateral retinaculum and presents with knee pain when climbing stairs or with prolonged sitting.</p><p>Referred pain from the hip (Choice K) may occur in the setting of hip injury or arthritis. Pain is typically unilateral and accompanied by limping and refusal to bear weight.</p><p>Reflex sympathetic dystrophy (Choice L) is a form of chronic nerve pain that presents with chronic burning pain of an extremity and with associated changes to the skin.</p><p>Rheumatoid arthritis (Choice M), juvenile rheumatoid arthritis in pediatric patients, occurs before the age of 16 and classically presents with joint pain and inflammation along with extra-articular findings such as uveitis, lymphadenopathy, and rash.</p><p>Subluxation of the patella (Choice O) refers to partial dislocation of the patella, commonly as an acute sports injury in young athletes. Patients present with acute knee pain.</p><p>Toxic synovitis (Choice P) is transient inflammation of the synovium that commonly occurs in pediatric patients less than 10 years old and is often preceded by an upper respiratory infection or viral prodrome. It presents with inability to bear weight but not with sepsis.</p>",
"16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The authority for disclosure of medical information to children rests with the child's parents. In the event of a nondisclosure request, disclosures may only be made with parental consent.</p><p>The disclosure of a fatal prognosis to pediatric patients is extremely challenging and requires careful balancing of the ethical imperatives to tell the truth, to involve the child in care, and to integrate the family's wishes along with the child's development and mental capabilities. Legally, minors are not considered to be autonomous decision makers and authority to determine what is in the child's best interest lies with the child's parents. In the case of nondisclosure requests by parents, any disclosure to the child should only be made with parental involvement and consent. In response to this child's question, it is best to gauge what discussion has taken place with the child's parents and to probe the child's level of understanding.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Choice B is likely to create conflict between the child and the parents. It also implies that there is significant information being concealed from the patient.</p><p>Choices C and D are not truthful responses. While the clinician has an ethical obligation to respect the wishes of the parents, the clinician must also abide by the ethical imperative not to lie. A more careful deflection by asking probing questions is a better response to this child's question.</p><p>Choice E is patronizing and fails to acknowledge the substance of this child's legitimate question. Failing to acknowledge the question inhibits the child's ability to participate in or understand their own care.</p>",
"17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Esophageal atresia presents with an inability to feed and manage oral secretions. If present along with a tracheoesophageal fistula, the patient may present with gagging, coughing, respiratory distress, and cyanosis in the neonatal period. Inability to pass a nasogastric tube with it remaining coiled in the proximal esophagus on chest x-ray is diagnostic. Definitive management requires surgical repair.</p><p>Newborns with esophageal atresia typically present with the inability to feed, with associated gagging and an inability to manage oral secretions. As well, since the fetus is unable to swallow amniotic fluid, the pregnancy may be complicated by polyhydramnios. Esophageal atresia often occurs with a concomitant tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF). The presence of a TEF, depending on the subtype, potentially conveys an increased risk for aspiration pneumonia and respiratory distress. Initial evaluation requires an attempt at insertion of a nasogastric tube and evaluation of its position with a chest x-ray. If the nasogastric tube remains coiled within the esophagus, this suggests the presence of esophageal atresia. Air within the gastrointestinal tract immediately after birth and prior to feeding suggests the existence of a TEF distal to the esophageal atresia. Definitive management requires surgical repair. Additional screening for associated congenital malformations should occur.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Genetic testing (Choice A) can be used to evaluate for chromosomal disorders, such as Down syndrome. While esophageal atresia is associated with malformation syndromes, it is not commonly correlated with a specific genetic abnormality.</p><p>Ultrasonography of the abdomen (Choice B) can be used to evaluate for pyloric stenosis and intussusception, among other disorders. As the esophagus typically terminates in the thorax in the setting of esophageal atresia, ultrasonography of the abdomen does not play a role in its diagnosis.</p><p>CT scan of the chest (Choice C) could potentially outline the patient's anatomy and characterize the esophageal atresia; however, placement of a nasogastric tube and chest x-ray would effectively provide the diagnosis while exposing the patient to less radiation.</p><p>Laryngoscopy (Choice D) is the process of using a laryngoscope or camera to examine the larynx of the patient. As laryngoscopy does not evaluate the esophagus, it does not play a role in the evaluation of esophageal atresia.</p>",
"18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hodgkin disease is a type of lymphoma, predominantly B-cell in origin, that demonstrates a bimodal age distribution. It typically presents with lymphadenopathy, weight loss, night sweats, cough, and pruritus. Evaluation commonly shows mediastinal lymphadenopathy, as well as Reed-Sternberg cells on histology.</p><p>Hodgkin disease is a type of lymphoma, predominantly B-cell in origin, that occurs commonly in patients who are 20 to 30 and 55 to 65 years old. Patients typically present with lymphadenopathy, as well as nonspecific constitutional symptoms, such as fever, night sweats, weight loss, and pruritus. The most common sites of lymphadenopathy are the neck and mediastinum. Laboratory values typically demonstrate a normal leukocyte count, anemia, increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and increased serum ferritin concentration, which are related to the underlying systemic inflammation. Initial evaluation commonly includes a chest x-ray or CT scan, which will potentially outline the extent of the patient's mediastinal or hilar lymphadenopathy. Definitive diagnosis requires excisional lymph node biopsy, which will disclose Reed-Sternberg cells (atypical cells with bilobed nuclei), a large inflammatory infiltrate with eosinophilic predominance, and fibrosis. Hodgkin cells are typically CD15- and CD30-positive. The most common subtype is nodular sclerosing, whereas the worst prognosis occurs in the setting of the lymphocyte-depleted subtype. Risk factors for its development include Epstein-Barr infection, immunosuppression, and a family history of Hodgkin disease. Treatment includes chemotherapy and radiation therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cytomegalovirus infection (Choice A) is often asymptomatic but can cause a short febrile, flu-like illness in healthy patients. It can cause severe infections in immunocompromised patients. It does not present with generalized pruritus.</p><p>Sarcoidosis (Choice C) is a chronic granulomatous inflammatory condition that can affect multiple organ systems, especially the lungs and lymphatic system. It typically presents with a cough, dyspnea, and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, and potentially with anterior uveitis, erythema nodosum, and arthralgias.</p><p>Thymoma (Choice D) is a benign or malignant tumor of the thymus. It presents with an anterior mediastinal mass, which can cause chest pain or shortness of breath. It is commonly associated with myasthenia gravis. It does not often present with lymphadenopathy.</p><p>Tuberculosis (Choice E) is spread via airborne transmission and can present with a primary, latent, or reactivation pattern. Primary disease demonstrates subacute fever, weight loss, night sweats, cough, and malaise, whereas latent tuberculosis is commonly asymptomatic. Reactivation presents similarly to primary disease but occurs after a period of latency, typically affecting the lung apices or resulting in miliary disease.</p>",
"19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and can be idiopathic or triggered by a recent infection or immune stimulus. Patients classically present with dependent edema in the buttocks, lower back, and lower extremities, foamy or dark-colored urine, hypoalbuminemia, and proteinuria. Minimal change disease appears normal on light microscopy.</p><p>Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by edema secondary to excessive proteinuria resulting in hypoalbuminemia and diminished intravascular oncotic pressure. This occurs because of loss of normal size and charge filtration of the glomerular capillary and Bowman capsule interface. Causes of nephrotic syndrome include minimal change disease, membranous nephropathy, and focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and can be idiopathic or triggered by a recent infection or immune stimulus. Patients classically present with dependent edema in the buttocks, lower back, and legs, foamy or dark-colored urine, hypoalbuminemia, and proteinuria. Minimal change disease appears normal on light microscopy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Acute glomerulonephritis (Choice A) can refer to a variety of glomerular diseases that involve inflammation of glomeruli, which typically present with hematuria, red blood cell urine casts, and hypertension. The patient has severe proteinuria and no RBCs in his urine, making this diagnosis unlikely.</p><p>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) (Choice B) is a cause of nephrotic syndrome that can result in proteinuria and pitting edema. FSGS is commonly associated with sickle cell disease, opioid use, and HIV, and is characterized by segmental sclerosis of the glomeruli that is visible on light microscopy.</p><p>IgA nephropathy (Choice C) is characterized by episodic hematuria that occurs concurrently with upper respiratory tract infections. When IgA deposition occurs in the renal mesangium, glomerulonephritis may ensue, causing microscopic hematuria, erythrocyte casts, and mild to moderate proteinuria.</p><p>Membranous nephropathy (Choice D) is a nephrotic syndrome which is more common in adults and can be secondary to drugs (eg, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs), infections (eg, hepatitis B and C), or systemic lupus erythematosus. Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.</p>",
"20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Aspiration pneumonia is typically secondary to impaired airway reflexes, abnormal oropharyngeal or tracheal anatomy or function, or severe gastroesophageal reflux with regurgitation of gastric contents. Recurrent aspiration from severe gastroesophageal reflux can be treated with fundoplication, which tightens the gastroesophageal sphincter and decreases the frequency and severity of reflux.</p><p>The inhalation of orogastric contents is known as aspiration, and it can occur from simple fluids such as water, or from stomach contents as a result of emesis or gastroesophageal reflux, as in this patient. The aspiration of acidic stomach contents may cause aspiration pneumonitis, whereas the aspiration of gastrointestinal flora and bacteria can cause aspiration pneumonia. The volume of aspirate is a strong predictor of complications from aspiration, especially in the case of pneumonia, as the bacterial inoculum may overwhelm the native ability of pulmonary alveolar defense (macrophages, cilia, mucus) to prevent colonization, replication, and infection. Aspiration pneumonia classically presents with a cough productive of foul-smelling sputum, fever, chills, chest pain, shortness of breath, and rhonchi on physical examination. Imaging classically shows an infiltrate in the most dependent lung segments at the time of aspiration (eg, in the superior segment of the inferior lobe when supine or lower lobes when erect). Patients at a higher risk for aspiration include those with impaired airway-protective reflexes (eg, neurological injury, myasthenia gravis, altered mental status), frequent or forceful emesis, abnormal pharyngeal or airway anatomy or function (eg, malignancy, tracheoesophageal fistula), or severe gastroesophageal reflux. Options for the management of severe gastroesophageal reflux in children include dietary changes and proton pump inhibitors or histamine-2 receptor antagonists, although these would not assist in decreasing the risk for aspiration pneumonia. Fundoplication is a surgical procedure that improves the gastroesophageal sphincter tone by wrapping the fundus around the distal esophagus, which decreases the frequency and severity of reflux.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Esophagostomy (Choice A) is a surgical opening into the esophagus that is occasionally used in head and neck cancer to provide enteral nutrition. It does not play a role in the treatment of gastroesophageal reflux.</p><p>Gastrostomy (Choice C) is the surgical creation of an opening into the stomach, which can be used for enteral nutrition or to remove the contents of the stomach via drainage. For this to be effective in preventing aspiration caused by gastroesophageal reflux, the placed tube would have to be placed to suction or to drainage at all times, which would prohibit proper nutrition. Fundoplication is a more reasonable option.</p><p>Pyloromyotomy (Choice D) is a procedure that decreases the strength of the pyloric sphincter. It is used to treat hypertrophic pyloric stenosis but is not used in the management of severe gastroesophageal reflux.</p><p>Vagotomy and pyloroplasty (Choice E) entails the surgical ligation of the vagus nerve, in addition to the enlargement of the pyloric opening, which is used to treat gastric ulcers. These procedures would not prevent aspiration in this patient.</p>",
"21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is a disorder marked by hypertrophy of the pylorus that can result in gastric outlet obstruction. A hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis results from the loss of gastric acid and potassium ions, along with contraction alkalosis caused by the severe volume depletion.</p><p>Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (IHPS) is a disorder of the pyloric sphincter that occurs in young infants and can result in partial or complete gastric outlet obstruction. It is caused by hypertrophy of the pylorus and an inability to relax the pyloric sphincter. Infants classically present within the first 1 to 2 months of life with forceful nonbilious emesis. Physical examination is notable for a characteristic ovoid mass in the epigastric region. The recurrent emesis, if severe, may result in signs of volume depletion and malnutrition. Laboratory studies typically show a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis caused by the loss of gastric hydrochloric acid and potassium ions. Additionally, the resultant volume depletion causes activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, resulting in renal losses of potassium and protons. Confirmatory diagnosis is made by ultrasound imaging to evaluate the thickness and length of the pyloric muscle. Patients are treated with surgical pyloromyotomy to relieve the stenosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Choices A and B are incorrect as they represent metabolic alkalosis with a high chloride concentration. Metabolic alkalosis that results from the loss of gastric acid typically results in hypochloremia as a result of the loss of chloride ions.</p><p>Choices D and E are incorrect as they represent hypochloremic acidosis and not an alkalosis as would be expected with the high-volume loss of gastric acid and contraction alkalosis. There are numerous causes of metabolic acidosis, and the differential can be narrowed by evaluating for a normal or high anion gap.</p>",
"22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neuroblastoma is a malignancy of neuroendocrine cells of the sympathetic nervous system that commonly affects the adrenal glands. It may be differentiated from other common abdominal malignancies of childhood by its tendency to cross the midline and have a nodular texture, along with its association with paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome and increased concentrations of serum and urine catecholamine metabolites.</p><p>Neuroblastoma is a neoplasm of neuroendocrine cells of the sympathetic nervous system. It may arise from any sympathetic nervous tissue and is the most common tumor of the adrenal gland that occurs in children. Other locations include the sympathetic chain. Adrenal involvement classically presents with abdominal pain and distention, weight loss, and failure to thrive. Patients may also present with an associated paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome. Physical examination may show a firm, nodular mass that crosses the midline and diastolic hypertension. Diagnosis is suggested by the presence of increased catecholamine metabolites in the serum and urine. Neuroblastoma is associated with overamplification of the MYCN oncogene. Histologic characteristics include small round blue cells that may form rosette patterns clustered around a central lumen, with positive stains for bombesin and neuron-specific enolase markers. Neuroblastoma commonly metastasizes to bone and skin. Treatment and prognosis are dependent on the stage of the tumor at diagnosis and the presence or absence of MYCN amplification.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Ewing sarcoma (Choice A) is a primary bone malignancy driven by t(11;22) chromosomal translocation. On histology, Ewing sarcoma demonstrates multiple small, round, blue cells. X-rays may demonstrate elevated periosteum and layered neocortex formation. It often occurs in the long bones (eg, femur, tibia) and typically presents in adolescence.</p><p>Multicystic dysplastic kidney (Choice B) is a nonhereditary pediatric cystic renal disease that results from the formation of multiple renal cysts in utero. It is typically sporadic. It can affect one or both kidneys, with a higher incidence of bilateral involvement in females. The affected kidney is replaced by numerous cysts and retains no functioning renal tissue.</p><p>Nephroblastoma (Wilms tumor) (Choice D) is the most common renal malignancy in childhood. It occurs secondary to mutations in tumor suppressor genes WT1 or WT2. It is characterized by a large, often palpable, unilateral flank mass and hematuria. It does not typically cross midline and is often smooth to palpation rather than nodular.</p><p>Polycystic kidney disease (Choice E) refers to either autosomal recessive or dominant polycystic kidney disease. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease is a form of infantile cystic renal disease that manifests with the development of numerous small renal cysts that replace the normal renal parenchyma, resulting in end-stage kidney disease. It typically has a variable age of presentation during fetal life or childhood but can be a potential cause of oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia (Potter sequence) if it develops in utero.</p>",
"23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Posterior urethral valves are formed from an abnormal remnant of the Wolffian duct and can result in urinary tract obstruction. Signs and symptoms can include bladder distention, hydronephrosis, oligohydramnios, and a fetus that is small for gestational age.</p><p>Congenital urinary tract abnormalities include but are not limited to unilateral renal agenesis, fused kidneys, ureteral stricture and stenosis, duplex collecting system, posterior urethral valves, bladder agenesis, bladder exstrophy, hypo- or epispadias, and urethral strictures. Posterior urethral valves are formed from an abnormal remnant of the Wolffian duct and can result in urinary tract obstruction. This is often diagnosed on prenatal ultrasonography showing bladder distention, hydronephrosis, oligohydramnios, and a fetus that is small for gestational age. In this patient who is premature, underweight, and has not voided, a midline mass is likely a distended bladder secondary to posterior urethral valves that were not diagnosed prenatally. Complications include rupture of a calyx around the kidney, or intraperitoneal bladder rupture. Definitive diagnosis of posterior urethral valves is made with voiding cystourethrography showing dilation, elongation, and tapering of the urethra. Treatment is with endoscopic valve ablation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and F.</b></p><p>Alcohol toxicity (Choice A) in pregnancy can result in fetal alcohol syndrome, which can cause intellectual disability and congenital abnormalities such as microcephaly, facial abnormalities (eg, thin vermillion border, small palpebral fissures), and heart defects.</p><p>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) (Choice B) defines a variable state of disease related to adrenal hypofunction that results in decreased concentrations of cortisol with variable effects on mineralocorticoids and androgens. The presentation varies depending on the age of onset, severity, and gender of the patient. In rare, mild cases, infertility may be the only presenting complaint when declared in adolescence or young adulthood. Most cases of CAH are diagnosed in childhood.</p><p>Meconium ileus (Choice C) occurs when abnormally thick meconium occludes the intestine, resulting in a bowel obstruction. It is associated with cystic fibrosis. This patient has already passed meconium.</p><p>Nephroblastoma (Wilms tumor) (Choice D) is the most common renal malignancy in childhood resulting from mutations in tumor suppressor genes WT1 or WT2. It is characterized by a large, often palpable, unilateral flank mass and hematuria.</p><p>Testicular torsion (Choice F) occurs when the testicle rotates about itself within the scrotal sac. The twisting of the spermatic cord, which contains the gonadal artery, pampiniform plexus, and gonadal vein, results in vascular congestion and potential cessation of venous or arterial flow. This can lead to testicular infarction if not promptly managed.</p>",
"24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pleural effusions may be categorized as transudative or exudative based on the Light criteria. Increased protein or cells in the fluid indicates an exudative effusion. Malignancy is a common cause of exudative pleural effusions, with lymphoma the most likely cause in a child with an underlying mediastinal mass.</p><p>The patient is most likely presenting with a malignant pleural effusion. His physical examination findings of jugular venous distention and edema of the face and upper extremities are suggestive of superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome, which is caused by obstruction of the SVC and venous congestion of the upper body and face. Obstruction of the SVC is often caused by extrinsic compression by a malignant mediastinal or pulmonary mass, which may be secondary to lung cancer, lymphoma, or metastatic disease from another primary source. Pleural effusions are clinically suspected on examination in the setting of dullness to percussion and diminished breath sounds, and can be visualized on imaging including chest x-ray, CT scan, or ultrasonography. Analyzing the pleural fluid can show an underlying cause to guide therapy. Fluid is inspected for cell counts, organisms, pH, enzyme activity, and serum glucose concentration and total protein concentrations. Low pH and low serum glucose are indicative of increased cellular metabolism in the fluid and may indicate underlying malignancy and/or infection. Malignant pleural effusions may have increased erythrocyte counts or appear grossly hemorrhagic as a result of tumor invasion and disruption of blood vessels and the visceral pleura. As well, Light criteria assesses whether the pleural effusion is likely to be exudative (eg, complex fluid resulting from pneumonia, empyema, malignancy, or inflammatory disease) or transudative (eg, serous fluid resulting from imbalanced hydrostatic or oncotic pressures) through evaluating the concentrations of pleural fluid protein and lactate dehydrogenase.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Congestive heart failure (Choice A) and nephrotic syndrome (Choice C) may be complicated by transudative pleural effusions caused by volume overload. Pleural effusions associated with volume overload are most likely bilateral as a result of the underlying systemic derangement, while exudative effusions related to malignancy or infection are more commonly unilateral. As well, transudative effusions do not demonstrate increased erythrocyte, lactate dehydrogenase, or protein concentrations.</p><p>Traumatic hemothorax (Choice D) is an exudative effusion that contains increased erythrocyte concentrations and may appear frankly bloody on thoracentesis. It often occurs in the setting of rib fractures and/or pulmonary contusions. Malignant pleural effusion is more likely in the setting of a mediastinal mass on imaging.</p><p>Tuberculous pleural effusion (Choice E) may be seen in endemic regions in the setting of chronic tuberculosis infection. The pleural fluid is characteristically exudative, with a high lymphocyte count and an increased adenosine deaminase concentration.</p>",
"25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Drain cleaners contain caustic alkali that can cause burns and ulcerations of the tongue, airway, and esophagus, resulting in stridor, hoarseness, vomiting, and drooling.</p><p>Drain cleaners are a common cause of caustic ingestions in children. They contain sodium and potassium hydroxides, alkali that are proton acceptors and strong nucleophiles. Alkali ingestion leads to liquefactive necrosis, lactic acidosis, and tissue penetration and ulceration. It can cause chemical burns and ulcerations of the tongue, airway, and esophagus, resulting in stridor, hoarseness, vomiting, and drooling. If severe, esophageal perforation can result leading to free air in the mediastinum. Treatment is initially focused on supportive measures including airway management and fluid resuscitation. Visible lesions in the oropharynx increase the likelihood of distal gastrointestinal lesions. Patients should be evaluated with endoscopy to assess for visceral injury. For severe injuries, treatment involves steroid administration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Acetaminophen (Choice A) toxicity is characterized by acute liver failure. At high doses, saturation of phase II metabolic pathways leads to excess acetaminophen being metabolized by cytochrome P450-mediated reactions to N-acetyl-p-benzoquinoneimine (NAPQI), which has strong oxidizing properties and is directly hepatotoxic.</p><p>Diphenhydramine (Choice B) ingestion results in central nervous system H1-receptor blockade, anticholinergic, and anti-α-adrenergic side effects. This results in a constellation of symptoms such as dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, hallucinations, delirium, ataxia, flushed skin, visual disturbances (cycloplegia), and sedation.</p><p>Ibuprofen (Choice D) is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that inhibits cyclooxygenase. In small doses, it can cause impaired gastric mucosal protection resulting in gastritis or peptic ulcer disease. In large overdose or ingestion, ibuprofen can cause hypotension, hypothermia, and metabolic acidosis; however, this is uncommon.</p><p>Organophosphates (Choice E) are found in insecticides; exposure can occur transcutaneously, or via inhalation or ingestion. They are a cause of cholinergic excess as they inhibit acetylcholinesterase, which is characterized by vomiting, diarrhea, urination, diaphoresis, salivation, lacrimation, bronchorrhea, bronchospasm, bradycardia, seizures, and muscle weakness.</p><p>Pseudoephedrine (Choice F) is a sympathomimetic that activates the adrenergic receptor system and is used as an over-the-counter nasal decongestant because of its effects on nasal vasoconstriction. Ingestion and toxicity can cause nausea, vomiting, dizziness, headache, restlessness, and anxiety.</p>",
"26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Transient synovitis is a noninfectious arthropathy that presents with pain of weight-bearing joints, often the hip, frequently following a viral infection in a child. Diagnosis involves the exclusion of emergent or urgent conditions such as cellulitis, septic arthritis, osteomyelitis, and fracture. It is best managed with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medications.</p><p>The most likely diagnosis in this otherwise healthy patient is transient synovitis of the hip. Transient synovitis is an inflammatory condition involving the synovial lining of weight-bearing joints, with the hip being commonly affected. It classically presents in children recovering from a viral infection with pain on weight-bearing, antalgic gait, refusal to ambulate, tenderness over the joint, and pain elicited with range of motion. Vital signs are generally within normal limits, as are laboratory studies with the exception of increased markers of inflammation such as erythrocyte sedimentation rate. Antecedent viral infection has been reported in up to 30% of cases of transient synovitis. Diagnosis involves the exclusion of emergent or urgent conditions such as cellulitis, septic arthritis, osteomyelitis, and fracture. Treatment is supportive, and involves rest plus nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medications, making oral ibuprofen the best choice for this patient. If there is diagnostic uncertainty, arthrocentesis to exclude septic arthritis, along with imaging to assess for osteomyelitis or fracture should be conducted.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Oral acyclovir (Choice A) is appropriate for cases of herpetic infections, such as herpes labialis, genital herpes, or varicella infections. Acyclovir is a guanosine analog that must first be activated by viral thymidine kinase, and ultimately prevents replication of the viral genome. It demonstrates no independent anti-inflammatory properties.</p><p>Oral clindamycin (Choice B), an antibiotic that inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit, is appropriate for treating cases of skin and soft-tissue infections, and exhibits broad activity against many gram-positive, gram-negative, and anaerobic pathogens.</p><p>Intravenous ceftriaxone (Choice D) and intravenous vancomycin (Choice E) are appropriate for the inpatient treatment of bacterial infections and are a common empiric regimen used to treat meningitis. Ceftriaxone, a third-generation cephalosporin, has a broad spectrum of activity against gram-positive and gram-negative organisms, including Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a common cause of migratory polyarthritis. Vancomycin inhibits the synthesis of bacterial cell walls, and therefore has the greatest activity against gram-positive organisms. It would be an appropriate choice to treat osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, or skin and soft-tissue infections caused by methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus.</p>",
"27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome) results from monosomy of the X chromosome and presents with characteristic physical features including short stature, a wide webbed neck, a broad chest with widely spaced nipples, multiple melanocytic nevi, primary amenorrhea, and delayed development of secondary sexual characteristics. Short stature in patients with Turner syndrome likely results from haploinsufficiency of the short stature homeobox gene on the X chromosome.</p><p>Gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome) results from monosomy of the X chromosome and presents with characteristic physical features including short stature, a wide webbed neck, a broad chest with widely spaced nipples, and multiple melanocytic nevi. Short stature in patients with Turner syndrome likely results from haploinsufficiency of the short stature homeobox gene on the X chromosome. Delayed development of secondary sexual characteristics is common, such as with the Tanner stage 1 breast and pubic hair development in this patient. Primary amenorrhea is also a common finding. Patients with Turner syndrome may also present with bicuspid aortic valve, aortic coarctation, or a fused kidney.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and F.</b></p><p>Chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis (Hashimoto disease) (Choice A) may occur in pediatric patients and more commonly presents with symptoms of hypothyroidism. Thyroid dysfunction is less likely to result in delayed development of secondary sexual characteristics.</p><p>Constitutional short stature (Choice B) may lead to short stature during childhood and adolescence but usually results in normal adult stature. This diagnosis would not account for the patient's finding of primary amenorrhea and delayed sexual development.</p><p>Craniopharyngioma (Choice C) may lead to acquired deficiency of growth hormone. However, other accompanying findings are usually encountered, such as headache, vomiting, and visual field defects.</p><p>Familial short stature (Choice D) is a common cause of short stature, but the child's expected height should be relatively similar to that of her parents. This child is both significantly shorter than both parents and has other findings, such as primary amenorrhea and delayed sexual development that would not be explained by this diagnosis.</p><p>Idiopathic hypopituitarism (Choice F) may result in deficiency of one or several pituitary hormones, but it is a diagnosis requiring laboratory investigation and would not present with the remaining characteristic physical examination findings of Turner syndrome.</p>",
"28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Nephroblastoma is the most common pediatric renal malignancy and is characterized by a large, often palpable, unilateral flank mass. The preferred initial diagnostic study is typically abdominal ultrasonography to evaluate the mass.</p><p>Nephroblastoma, or Wilms tumor, is the most common renal malignancy in childhood as a result of mutations in tumor suppressor genes WT1 or WT2. It is characterized by a large, often palpable, unilateral flank mass. Patients can be asymptomatic or have symptoms of abdominal pain or hematuria. Nephroblastoma can also be associated with congenital anomalies such as hemihypertrophy, genitourinary abnormalities, and aniridia. The preferred initial diagnostic study is typically abdominal ultrasonography to evaluate the abdominal mass. Other masses that can be differentiated from nephroblastoma include hydronephrosis and polycystic kidney disease. If nephroblastoma is diagnosed, further imaging can be done to further evaluate any tumor infiltration and the full extent of the mass.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum catecholamine concentration (Choice A) can be performed in the evaluation of pheochromocytoma or neuroblastoma. Measurement of urine vanillylmandelic acid and homovanillic acid concentrations (Choice B) is also performed in the evaluation of pheochromocytoma. Pheochromocytoma presents with episodic headaches, sweating, palpitations, tremors, agitation, tachycardia, tachypnea, and treatment-refractory hypertension. It is a common tumor of the adrenal medulla and is marked by the functional secretion of epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dopamine, which cause episodic hyperadrenergic symptoms.</p><p>X-ray of the abdomen (Choice C) can be performed to evaluate for bowel obstruction, swallowed foreign body, necrotizing enterocolitis, or constipation. An x-ray is not as sensitive as a CT scan and has little utility in the evaluation of abdominal masses.</p><p>Chest imaging can elucidate whether there are any lung metastases. An x-ray of the chest (Choice D) would be less sensitive for this purpose, and the abdominal mass should be first evaluated.</p>",
"29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is a nephritic syndrome characterized by peripheral and periorbital edema, dark-colored urine, hypertension, acute kidney injury, and proteinuria following an infection with Streptococcus pyogenes (group A). The pathogenesis of PSGN involves streptococcal antigens complexing with antibodies, deposition of immune complexes in the glomerular basement membrane, and subsequent complement activation and inflammation.</p><p>Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is a type of nephritic syndrome characterized by peripheral and periorbital edema, dark-colored urine, hypertension, acute kidney injury, and proteinuria following an infection with Streptococcus pyogenes (group A). The pathogenesis of PSGN involves streptococcal antigens complexing with antibodies, deposition of immune complexes in the glomerular basement membrane, and subsequent complement activation and inflammation. Neutrophilic infiltration of the glomerular tuft can be seen in PSGN. Management of PSGN is supportive and focused on treating complications such as volume overload and hypertension with diuretics and antihypertensive medications. Rarely, severe kidney injury secondary to PSGN may require kidney replacement therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>The pathogenesis of poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis involves antigen-antibody immune complex deposition, not direct bacterial infiltration of the basement membrane or epithelial cells (Choices B and C).</p><p>Destruction of tubular epithelial cells (Choice D) describes the pathophysiology of acute tubular necrosis. Acute tubular necrosis typically occurs following an ischemic (Choice E) or nephrotoxic insult to the kidneys, which results in necrosis of the tubular epithelium. Granular, muddy brown casts are typical on urinalysis.</p>",
"30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Trisomy 18, also known as Edwards syndrome, presents with characteristic physical features including intellectual disability, a prominent occiput, low-set ears, micrognathia, clenched overlapping fingers, and feet with prominent calcanei and convexly rounded soles.</p><p>Trisomy 18, also known as Edwards syndrome, presents with characteristic physical features including intellectual disability, a prominent occiput, low-set ears, micrognathia, clenched overlapping fingers, and feet with prominent calcanei and convexly rounded soles. It is the second most common trisomy in infants and is associated with decreased serum PAPP-A and β-human chorionic gonadotropin concentrations in the first trimester. Treatment involves correction or management of life-threatening abnormalities. Most patients die within the first 6 months of age. In most cases, it is caused by a meiotic nondisjunction, which occurs when two chromatids fail to separate and a single haploid gamete contains two copies of a chromosome instead of one. The incidence of meiotic nondisjunction increases with increasing maternal age.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Arthrogryposis (Choice A) describes congenital joint contractures, which result in impaired joint movement and function and may lead to muscle atrophy.</p><p>Congenital infection (Choice B) can occur with multiple viruses such as cytomegalovirus (CMV), herpes simplex virus (HSV), rubella, measles, or parvovirus. Each congenital viral infection results in different congenital abnormalities. HSV can cause both cutaneous herpetic lesions and systemic manifestations like meningoencephalitis. Congenital infection with CMV manifests as hearing loss, seizures, a petechial rash, intracranial calcifications, organomegaly, and lymphadenopathy. Neonates with congenital rubella infection exhibit a classic triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease.</p><p>Exposure to alcohol in utero (Choice C) results in fetal alcohol syndrome, which can cause intellectual disability and congenital abnormalities such as microcephaly, facial abnormalities (eg, thin vermillion border, small palpebral fissures), and heart defects.</p><p>Uteroplacental insufficiency (Choice E) describes insufficient blood flow to the placenta, abnormal placenta, or inability of the placenta to deliver sufficient nutrients to the fetus during pregnancy. It can result in a variety of disorders including fetal hypoglycemia, oligohydramnios, intrauterine growth restriction, or maternal complications including preeclampsia, miscarriage, or stillbirth.</p>",
"31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potential bloody, mucoid stools.</p><p>Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potential bloody, mucoid stools. Classically, it presents in an infant or young child with recurrent episodes of severe abdominal pain often punctuated by asymptomatic periods. Intussusception typically occurs as a result of the presence of a pathologic lead point, often lymphoid hyperplasia, lymphadenopathy, Meckel diverticulum, stricture, or malignancy, which acts to draw one segment of bowel into an adjacent segment during peristalsis. If the intussusception self-resolves, there may be no symptoms. If it does not resolve, traction on mesenteric vessels can result in hypoperfusion of the segment with resultant inflammation, hemorrhage, obstruction, and pain. It may be detected on physical examination as an elongated mass, often in the right upper quadrant. Diagnosis consists of clinical history and physical examination and is supported by imaging. Ultrasonography evaluation is preferred in young children and will show telescoping interposed bowel segments in longitudinal orientation or a targetoid appearance in transverse orientation. Treatment of an ileocolic intussusception requires the administration of a contrast enema, which decreases the intussusception. Contrast enema is both diagnostic and therapeutic in this setting. Surgery may be required in cases where there is repetitive failure of the enema to decrease the intussusception or if the patient presents with symptoms concerning for bowel necrosis or perforation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Gastritis (Choice A) can be caused by a viral illness or use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It is characterized by epigastric discomfort, nausea, vomiting, or if severe, hematemesis. The associated pain is typically constant.</p><p>Juvenile polyps (Choice C) are benign hamartomas that can present with painless rectal bleeding. Abdominal pain would be uncommon. In a patient with severe abdominal cramping followed by bloody stool, intussusception is more likely.</p><p>Peptic ulcer disease (PUD) (Choice D) is uncommon in children and can be related to NSAIDs or Helicobacter pylori infection. Patients typically present with epigastric discomfort, nausea, and vomiting, typically after eating. PUD can be complicated by hemorrhage or perforation.</p><p>Viral gastroenteritis (Choice E) is a common, typically self-limiting illness in children characterized by nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and mild abdominal discomfort. It would usually not cause bloody stools. Bloody diarrhea in children should raise suspicion for bacterial gastroenteritis by Escherichia coli O157:H7.</p>",
"32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In boys, puberty follows a predictable course of gonadarche, pubarche, and growth spurt. Delayed puberty is defined as the absence of testicular enlargement or secondary sexual characteristics at the age of 14 years and is most commonly caused by constitutional delay of growth and puberty.</p><p>Puberty in boys typically follows a predictable course of gonadarche (enlargement of the testes), followed by pubarche (pubic hair), followed by adrenarche (axillary hair, facial hair, voice changes), followed by a growth spurt. This differs from the process in girls, which proceeds from thelarche (breast buds) to pubarche to growth spurt and finally menarche. In boys, delayed puberty is defined as the absence of testicular enlargement or secondary sexual characteristics at the age of 14 years. The most common cause of delayed puberty is constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP), accounting for approximately 50% of cases, a normal variant. In CDGP, there is a transient defect in the production of gonadotropin-releasing hormone with resultant delay in the stimulation of pituitary production of follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone. CDGP tends to be familial with many members of the same family undergoing late puberty compared with peers. In this case, the patient has undergone gonadarche (indicated by his Tanner stage 2 genital development) and pubarche but has not yet undergone adrenarche. Thus, while delayed, the sequence of his procession through the steps of puberty is appropriate and he can be reassured that his development will continue to progress.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum testosterone concentration (Choice B) is unnecessary in this case because the patient is progressing through the stages of puberty as expected. If his puberty did not continue to progress, testosterone measurement could be used to differentiate CDGP from another pathology.</p><p>Mild bilateral gynecomastia can be normal in early puberty as a result of the small increase in extragonadal production of estrogen relative to the amount of testosterone being produced. Urine toxicology screening (Choice C) would be appropriate if there was a suspicion for drug use inducing gynecomastia, of which marijuana is the most common recreational drug. Breast biopsy (Choice E) would be indicated if the breast enlargement was caused by a nodule or mass rather than true gynecomastia.</p><p>If delayed puberty were accompanied by neurologic findings or suspected to be caused by a process involving the brain such as a pituitary adenoma, MRI of the brain, not CT scan of the head (Choice D), would be indicated. Neither imaging modality is necessary in this case.</p>",
"33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Tinea capitis presents commonly in children with scaly patches, hair loss, and cervical lymphadenopathy. A frequent causal organism is Trichophyton tonsurans.</p><p>Tinea capitis is a fungal infection of the scalp that is common in children. It presents as scaly patches with alopecia, patches of alopecia with black dots that represent broken hairs, and cervical lymphadenopathy. The most common causal organism is Trichophyton tonsurans, although infections can also be caused by other Trichophyton species, Microsporum species, and Epidermophyton species. A potassium hydroxide preparation of a skin scraping will show fungal hyphae or spores. It is treated with oral antifungals, such as griseofulvin or terbinafine.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Candida albicans (Choice A) can cause a variety of infections, such as oral thrush, esophagitis, and vulvovaginal candidiasis. It can also cause systemic infections in immunocompromised patients. It does not cause tinea capitis.</p><p>Epidermophyton floccosum (Choice B) is a dermatophyte that can cause skin and nail infections, such as tinea pedis and tinea corporis. It less commonly causes tinea capitis.</p><p>Malassezia furfur (Choice C) causes tinea versicolor, a superficial fungal infection of the skin that commonly presents with areas of hypopigmentation, hyperpigmentation, or macules on the trunk and proximal upper extremities. It does not cause tinea capitis.</p><p>Microsporum audouinii (Choice D) is a dermatophyte that causes skin infections, such as tinea capitis and tinea corporis. However, it is a less common causal pathogen than Trichophyton tonsurans in tinea capitis.</p>",
"34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Defense against varicella zoster virus relies on T lymphocyte function, which is impaired in the setting of HIV infection and T-lymphocyte immunodeficiency. Infants with deficient T lymphocyte function are at an increased risk for severe infection with varicella, which may manifest as persistent infection or end organ involvement.</p><p>Varicella zoster, also known as human herpesvirus-3, is transmitted via respiratory secretions and direct contact with infected skin lesions. It causes the disease chickenpox in immunocompetent children and can cause encephalitis and pneumonia in immunosuppressed children. The rash associated with chickenpox typically persists for 1 to 2 weeks. Varicella is uncommon in infancy and may occur as an opportunistic infection in infants with HIV or as an immunocompromising condition impairing T lymphocytes. As with many viral infections, T lymphocyte function is an essential component of host defense and is impaired in the setting of HIV infection. Persistence of symptoms for 1 month is suggestive of underlying T-lymphocyte immunodeficiency. Infants with immunodeficiency are at a greater risk for severe consequences of varicella infection, including end organ involvement.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and F.</b></p><p>Impaired B-lymphocyte function (Choice A) occurs in several immunodeficiencies such as X-linked agammaglobulinemia and common variable immunodeficiency. Affected infants demonstrate recurrent infections with both bacterial and viral organisms.</p><p>Impaired complement function (Choice B) occurs in several complement disorders. Patients with deficient function of complement are at increased risk for infection with encapsulated bacterial organisms.</p><p>Impaired neutrophil adhesion (Choice C) occurs in leukocyte adhesion deficiency. Patients present with recurrent skin and mucosal infections, absent pus, and delayed separation of the umbilical cord.</p><p>Impaired neutrophil chemotaxis (Choice D) occurs in Chediak-Higashi syndrome, which presents with albinism, peripheral neuropathy, and recurrent pyogenic infections.</p><p>Neutrophil oxidation burst (Choice F) defects occur in chronic granulomatous disease, which presents with recurrent infections with organisms possessing catalase, such as Staphylococcus aureus.</p>",
"35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neurofibromatosis 1 is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous disorder that typically results from an inherited mutation of the NF1 tumor suppressor gene. The disorder presents with cutaneous neurofibromas (benign neoplasms derived from neural crest cells), cafe-au-lait spots, pigmented iris hamartomas (Lisch nodules), optic gliomas, and/or pheochromocytomas.</p><p>Neurofibromatosis 1 is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous disorder that typically results from an inherited mutation of the NF1 tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 17. Neurofibromatosis 1 is inherited with complete penetrance and variable expressivity. Rarely, the mutation in the NF1 gene occurs sporadically. The disorder presents with cutaneous neurofibromas (benign neoplasms derived from neural crest cells), cafe-au-lait spots, axillary or inguinal freckling, pigmented iris hamartomas (Lisch nodules), optic gliomas, and/or pheochromocytomas. The diagnosis is typically based on physical examination findings, but genetic testing may be utilized to confirm ambiguous cases. Treatment includes surgical debulking of tumors, management of seizures and neurological complications, and genetic counseling.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Autosomal recessive neurocutaneous disorders (Choice B) include ataxia-telangiectasia, which presents with oculocutaneous telangiectasias, progressive cerebellar ataxia, immune deficiency, and neurologic abnormalities.</p><p>Mitochondrially inherited disorders (Choice C) rarely include neurocutaneous findings. Mitochondrial diseases typically present with muscle weakness, encephalopathy, and incoordination.</p><p>The inheritance pattern of neurofibromatosis 1 is not multifactorial (Choice D) or unknown (Choice F). It is known to be inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with complete penetrance and variable expressivity.</p><p>X-linked recessive neurocutaneous disorders (Choice E) include Menkes disease, a congenital X-linked disorder that causes a defect in intestinal copper absorption, leading to copper deficiency. The disease presents with progressive neurologic symptoms, hypopigmentation of the skin, bony abnormalities, and kinked hair.</p>",
"36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Viral myocarditis can present in children with fever and signs and symptoms characteristic of heart failure, including volume overload, chest pain, shortness of breath, pulmonary edema noted as crackles auscultated in the lungs, and an S3 gallop on physical examination.</p><p>Viral myocarditis can present in children with fever and signs and symptoms characteristic of heart failure, including volume overload, chest pain, shortness of breath, pulmonary edema noted as crackles auscultated in the lungs, and an S3 gallop on physical examination. In addition, patients may also present with arrhythmias, exercise intolerance, syncope, hypotension, tachypnea, and tachycardia. An S3 gallop is a heart sound associated with dilated cardiomyopathy and heart failure as a result of increased atrial and ventricular end-diastolic volume. Children may have a viral prodrome of fever, malaise, and myalgia or a recent respiratory or gastrointestinal illness prior to the onset of myocarditis. Treatment of children with myocarditis focuses on supportive care to maintain perfusion and hemodynamic stability, including diuretics and noninvasive or mechanical ventilation if necessary.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Bronchiolitis (Choice A) is a respiratory tract infection commonly caused by respiratory syncytial virus (RSV). Although it can cause respiratory distress as a result of mucous plugging of small airways, it does not cause volume overload, pulmonary edema, or an S3 gallop. Rhonchi, not crackles, are typically heard on lung auscultation.</p><p>Endocarditis (Choice B) is a rare disease in children that is usually associated with an underlying congenital heart disease, rheumatic heart disease, or central venous catheter use. It involves vegetations that form on the endocardium or valves and can present with fever, new murmur, vegetation seen on echocardiography, or blood cultures growing microorganisms typical of endocarditis.</p><p>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Choice C) can be asymptomatic, may present with syncope on exertion, or may present with signs of heart failure as in this patient, but typically in a subacute or chronic course. Patients may also have exertional chest pain or arrhythmias. On examination, a systolic murmur can be heard as a result of the left ventricular outflow tract obstruction. Fever and an S3 gallop are more suggestive of myocarditis.</p><p>Mitral valve rupture (Choice E) can lead to pulmonary edema, shortness of breath, and tachypnea. However, it is not commonly associated with fever unless it presents as a complication of endocarditis, and is characterized by a systolic murmur on auscultation, not an S3 gallop.</p>",
"37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Extracellular hyperglycemia in the setting of diabetic ketoacidosis creates an osmotic effect resulting in the shift of fluid from the intracellular to the extracellular space with accompanying diuresis and intracellular volume loss. Restoration of fluid balance is an essential step in the emergent management of diabetic ketoacidosis and is best accomplished with the intravenous administration of 0.9% saline.</p><p>Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) occurs as a result of the accumulation of serum ketones such as β-hydroxybutyric acid, acetoacetic acid, and acetone in the setting of deficient insulin signaling. Dysregulated fatty acid degradation and ketone synthesis occurs as a result of the relative state of starvation created through absent insulin signaling. Extracellular hyperglycemia creates an osmotic effect resulting in the shift of fluid from the intracellular to the extracellular space with accompanying profuse osmotic diuresis and intracellular volume loss. Patients present with signs and symptoms including tachycardia, tachypnea, fatigue, polyuria, shortness of breath, Kussmaul respiration, and diffuse abdominal discomfort. Laboratory evaluation typically shows increased concentrations of glucose, ketones, and a high anion-gap metabolic acidosis caused by the accumulation of unmeasured anions (ketones, lactic acid) in serum. Treatment is through the replacement of insulin, volume repletion, correction of electrolyte abnormalities, and treatment of any precipitating conditions. Insulin is typically repleted at an initial dose of 0.1 U/kg/h by intravenous infusion. Volume resuscitation with 0.9% saline is essential to restore intracellular fluid volume and lower the extracellular glucose concentration and is the initial step in the management of DKA even prior to insulin administration. Administering insulin prior to knowing the serum potassium concentration can result in life-threatening hypokalemia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Intravenous administration of 20 mL/kg of 5% dextrose in water (Choice A) would worsen this patient's profound hyperglycemia. 0.9% saline is a more appropriate initial resuscitation fluid.</p><p>Intravenous administration of 20 mL/kg of lactated Ringer solution with 40 mEq/L of potassium chloride (Choice C) may be acceptable once the patient's potassium concentration is known; however, in DKA, derangements in potassium can include hypo- or hyperkalemia. Infusing a fluid with potassium may result in worsening of unknown derangements with potentially life-threatening dysrhythmias.</p><p>Administration of regular insulin either via intravenous dose at 0.1 U/h (Choice D) or via continuous intravenous infusion at 0.1 U/h (Choice E) is not the best next step because neither of these options is adjusted for the patient's weight. Insulin is typically repleted at an initial dose of 0.1 U/kg/h by intravenous infusion. Intravenous volume repletion with 0.9% saline is therefore a more appropriate initial step. Additionally, insulin repletion is only appropriate in DKA once the serum potassium concentration is known to prevent precipitating life-threatening derangements in potassium.</p>",
"38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cushing syndrome presents with weight gain, hirsutism, abdominal striae, menstrual irregularity, and mood changes. The diagnosis is first established biochemically by demonstrating excess cortisol, and the source is then localized with a serum adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) concentration followed by imaging of the adrenal or pituitary gland.</p><p>This patient's increasing facial hair growth, weight gain, abdominal striae, irregular menses, and mood changes are suggestive of Cushing syndrome, which results from chronic exposure to excess glucocorticoids. Causes include exogenous glucocorticoid use, an adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)-secreting pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease), ectopic ACTH secretion by a tumor, and a cortisol-secreting adrenal tumor. Evaluation begins by confirming hypercortisolism biochemically. Other screening methods, such as a 24-hour urine free cortisol measurement, are preferable, but of the options available, measurement of a morning serum cortisol concentration is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis. Once hypercortisolism is confirmed, the cause is localized by measuring the serum ACTH concentration and then by imaging.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum prolactin concentration (Choice B) is used in the evaluation of hyperprolactinemia, which presents with oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea and galactorrhea. This patient has no galactorrhea, and her hirsutism, weight gain, and striae are better explained by cortisol excess.</p><p>Measurement of serum testosterone concentration (Choice C) is useful in the evaluation of an androgen-secreting tumor, which presents with virilization (eg, clitoromegaly). This patient has no clitoromegaly, and her hirsutism is accompanied by weight gain and striae, which suggest glucocorticoid excess.</p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice D) and MRI of the brain (Choice E) are used to localize the cause of Cushing syndrome once hypercortisolism has been confirmed. The serum ACTH concentration is measured first, followed by CT scan of the abdomen if an adrenal source is likely or MRI of the brain if a pituitary source is likely. The disease is diagnosed biochemically before its source is sought radiologically.</p>",
"39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rubeola is caused by the measles paramyxovirus and presents with an acute febrile illness accompanied by cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a confluent maculopapular rash that starts at the head/neck and spreads to the trunk, excluding the palms and soles.</p><p>Measles (rubeola) virus is a highly contagious paramyxovirus that causes the disease measles, which is associated with acute febrile illness and potentially severe sequelae. It is more common in children than adults. It characteristically presents with a prodromal fever along with cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a confluent maculopapular rash that starts at the head/neck and spreads to the trunk, excluding the palms and soles. Physical examination may show bright red macules with a bluish-white center on the buccal mucosa (Koplik spots) and lymphadenopathy. Treatment is supportive. Vitamin A deficiency is associated with severe disease and complications, and supplementation should be provided, especially in children who are malnourished. Complications of measles include subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (a neurodegenerative disease associated with intellectual regression, personality changes, motor deterioration, seizures, and premature death), encephalitis, and giant cell pneumonia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, H, I, and J.</b></p><p>Ehrlichiosis (Choice A) is a tick-borne illness with a wide spectrum of disease characteristics, including fever, malaise, myalgia, headache, nausea, vomiting, rash, and altered mental status.</p><p>Henoch-Schonlein purpura (Choice B) is an acute, systemic, small-vessel vasculitis that occurs in children and typically presents with gravity-dependent purpura (eg, buttocks, legs, and feet), arthralgias, hematuria, and abdominal pain. It often occurs following a viral illness or upper respiratory tract infection.</p><p>Id reaction (Choice C) is a manifestation of type IV (delayed) hypersensitivity reaction. It often presents as a diffuse eczematous rash triggered by inflammation of the skin at a single site, such as soles in the presence of tinea pedis or the hands in dyshidrotic eczema.</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice D) is a multisystemic vasculitic disease resulting in acute arteritis with a risk for the development of coronary artery aneurysms. In children, the disease is characterized by fever for at least five days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips.</p><p>Pityriasis rosea (Choice E) is a cutaneous eruption characterized by the acute onset of a pruritic patch followed by the development of numerous smaller macules and patches. It commonly involves the trunk and proximal extremities. It may be induced by a viral infection.</p><p>Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Choice F) is a tick-borne Rickettsial disease caused by the organism Rickettsia rickettsii, which presents with a petechial rash that starts at the ankles and wrists. It is classically accompanied by headache and fever, but not cough, coryza, or conjunctivitis.</p><p>Scabies (Choice H) is a skin infestation by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei that is transmissible by skin-to-skin contact, as in family members or sexual partners. The skin infestation typically occurs on the sides or webs of the fingers, wrists, axillae, areolae, or genitalia and leads to intense pruritus.</p><p>Scarlet fever (Choice I) can present with an erythematous tongue, rash, and desquamation of hands and feet. The rash of scarlet fever is characteristically a rough, papular, diffuse body rash.</p><p>Staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (Choice J) is characterized by a full-body, erythematous, desquamating rash with accentuation in the skin folds. It is caused by production of exfoliative toxin by Staphylococcus aureus.</p>",
"40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bronchiectasis is an obstructive lung disease characterized by dilation and thickening of the bronchi or bronchioles. It has numerous associated causes, including cystic fibrosis, Kartagener syndrome, and immunodeficiencies with recurrent necrotizing pulmonary infections. Chronic cough is a typical presenting symptom in children.</p><p>Bronchiectasis refers to dilation and distortion of the bronchi or bronchioles that leads to obstructive lung disease. There are numerous causes in children, including congenital bronchiectasis resulting from underlying genetic disorders of cartilage or the airways, cystic fibrosis, Kartagener syndrome, recurrent aspiration, airway malignancies, immunodeficiency syndromes with recurrent necrotizing pulmonary infections, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, and connective tissue disorders. Children classically present with chronic cough (with or without sputum production) and symptoms of infection (fever, dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain) during exacerbations. Exercise intolerance and dyspnea on exertion are usually absent until late in disease progression. Associated symptoms and physical examination findings are dependent on the underlying cause. Chest imaging may show linear atelectasis or scarring, dilated airways with thickened walls, and peripheral opacities related to multifocal mucous plugs. Bronchiectasis may result in a progressive decline in pulmonary function, if untreated, and manifest as a severe, chronic obstructive lung disease that limits a patient's activity. Lung transplantation may be required later in life.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>α1-Antitrypsin deficiency (Choice A) results in the early development of panacinar emphysema and cirrhosis. It is a hereditary disease resulting from misfolding of α1-antitrypsin. The lack of functional α1-antitrypsin release results in an imbalance of proteases and antiproteases in the lung parenchyma, resulting in the destruction of alveolar septa and development of panacinar emphysema.</p><p>Asthma (Choice B) is characterized by reversible obstruction to airflow caused by the spasm of smooth muscle in the bronchi and small airways, and may present with dyspnea, wheezing, or chronic nonproductive cough. It is often triggered by an allergen (eg, smoke, pollen), exercise, or temperature change. It may be associated with progressive reactive airway remodeling over time. The lack of response to inhaled β-adrenergic agonists and oral prednisone in this patient does not support the diagnosis of asthma.</p><p>Congenital emphysema (Choice D) is a rare pulmonary disorder caused by disrupted embryonic bronchopulmonary development and is characterized by air trapping and lung hyperinflation, which can be visualized on chest imaging. The severity of disease varies widely and may be asymptomatic until adulthood or present in the newborn with respiratory distress.</p><p>Cystic fibrosis (Choice E) is a clinical syndrome resulting from defects in the CFTR gene, leading to a deficiency in a chloride channel that secretes chloride in the lungs and gastrointestinal tract and reabsorbs chloride in sweat glands. Cystic fibrosis is associated with bronchiectasis because of the risk for recurrent pulmonary infections. The absence of other findings associated with cystic fibrosis, such as pancreatic insufficiency, diarrhea, malnutrition, and weight loss, argues against cystic fibrosis as the cause of this patient's bronchiectasis.</p>",
"41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) recommends screening all sexually active adolescent women under the age of 25 years for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae infections. Early identification of these often asymptomatic infections will prevent long-term sequelae from developing. Cervical ectropion is a normal variant and does not warrant treatment, although it does increase the risk for acquiring sexually transmitted infections such as Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, and HIV.</p><p>In this healthy, adolescent female, preventative measures will primarily address her sexual health. The US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) recommends screening all sexually active adolescent women under the age of 25 years for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection. Chlamydia trachomatis is a common sexually transmitted disease that can cause various infections including cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, epididymitis, and urethritis. However, in most cases it is asymptomatic and thus screening asymptomatic individuals is important for disclosing and treating active infections before long-term sequelae develop. Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae also commonly co-infect the same individual. This patient's examination demonstrates cervical ectropion, which develops when there is slight eversion of the cervix to expose a portion of the endocervix and its columnar mucosa to the vaginal canal. In young women, it is a normal variant and does not warrant treatment, although it does increase the risk for acquiring sexually transmitted infections such as Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, and HIV.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Human papillomavirus (HPV) testing (Choice A) is not indicated until a patient reaches the age of 30 years old. Prior to that time, cytologic study of cells collected from the cervix alone is adequate for the screening of cervical cancer. After the age of 30, cervical cancer screening may be performed by cytologic study every 3 years or by cytologic study combined with HPV testing every 5 years.</p><p>Measurement of serum follicle-stimulating and luteinizing hormone concentrations (Choice C) is unnecessary since this patient does not demonstrate signs of delayed puberty. There are no indications to suggest an abnormality in this patient's hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis.</p><p>Measurement of serum thyroid-stimulating hormone and free thyroxine concentrations (Choice D) is unnecessary and not indicated for screening in an asymptomatic individual. Measurement of these laboratory values would be indicated if the patient were showing signs or symptoms of hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism.</p><p>Colposcopy (Choice E) is a procedure in which the cervix is visualized under magnification for the purpose of identifying cancerous or precancerous areas to obtain diagnostic samples. Colposcopy is indicated as a follow-up to an abnormal cervical cancer screening test or to further evaluate abnormal findings on initial cervical examination. This patient's ectropion is common in adolescents and is considered a normal variant. There is no indication for colposcopy in this patient.</p>",
"42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The radial nerve is commonly injured in midshaft fractures of the humerus as it courses in the spiral groove. Radial nerve injuries typically present with the inability to extend the elbow, wrist, or fingers, along with the loss of sensation to the posterior arm and dorsal hand.</p><p>The radial nerve is a nerve of the brachial plexus with contributions from the C5-T1 nerve roots. It courses through the axilla, down the posterior shaft of the humerus within the spiral groove, and then along the posterolateral aspect of the forearm. It provides sensation to the posterior upper extremity and the dorsal hand, as well as motor sensation to the extensors of the elbow, wrist, and fingers. It is prone to injury with midshaft humerus fractures, as it runs in close proximity to the humeral diaphysis within the spiral groove.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>The musculocutaneous nerve (Choice A) courses along the anterolateral portion of the arm and is responsible for sensation over the lateral forearm, as well as elbow flexion and forearm supination. This patient is unable to extend his wrist and fingers, which is more typical of a radial nerve injury.</p><p>The posterior cord of the brachial plexus (Choice B) contributes to the subscapular, thoracodorsal, lower subscapular, axillary, and radial nerves. While an injury to the posterior cord of the brachial plexus would present with loss of wrist and finger extension, it would also be expected to cause weakness of the deltoid muscle and loss of sensation over the shoulder, in addition to other symptoms.</p><p>The median nerve (Choice C) courses along the anteromedial arm and through the carpal tunnel to the hand. It is responsible for sensation to the volar aspect of the lateral 3 ½ fingers, as well as wrist flexion, lateral finger flexion, and thumb opposition. It is most commonly injured in supracondylar fractures of the humerus given its course, not in midshaft humerus fractures.</p><p>The ulnar nerve (Choice E) courses along the medial aspect of the arm. It provides sensation to the volar aspect of the medial 1 ½ fingers, as well as wrist flexion, medial finger flexion, finger abduction, and finger adduction. It is most commonly injured in medial epicondyle fractures of the humerus, not midshaft fractures of the humerus.</p>",
"43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Benign neonatal hyperbilirubinemia, or physiologic jaundice, is caused by the increased breakdown of red blood cells and thus generation of bilirubin, the immature liver metabolism of bilirubin, and the increased enterohepatic circulation of bilirubin. Jaundice within the first 24 hours of life that rises faster than 0.5 mg/dL per hour and a predominance of direct bilirubin are features suggestive of a pathologic cause of hyperbilirubinemia.</p><p>Neonatal jaundice is defined as an increased bilirubin concentration (greater than 5 mg/dL), which results from either increased hemolysis (bilirubin generation) or decreased bilirubin conjugation or excretion. Hyperbilirubinemia can be divided into direct and indirect forms in which either conjugated or unconjugated bilirubin accumulate, respectively. It can also be divided into physiologic and pathologic forms. Features of physiologic jaundice include development after 24 hours of life, a rate of bilirubin rise of less than 0.5 mg/dL daily, a maximum peak of 14 to 15 mg/dL, and a predominance of indirect bilirubin (greater than 90%). In contrast, jaundice within the first 24 hours of life (which rises faster than 0.5 mg/dL per hour), a predominance of direct bilirubin, and persistence beyond 2 weeks would suggest a pathologic cause. Newborn physiologic jaundice, also termed benign neonatal hyperbilirubinemia, occurs in nearly all newborns and is caused by the increased breakdown of red blood cells and thus generation of bilirubin, the immature liver metabolism of bilirubin, and the increased enterohepatic circulation of bilirubin. These features taken together cause an indirect hyperbilirubinemia to develop. Neonatal hyperbilirubinemia is closely monitored in order to prevent kernicterus, which refers to the deposition of bilirubin in the basal ganglia, pons, and cerebellum. This can occur at bilirubin concentrations of 25 to 30 mg/dL. Treatment of the underlying cause of the jaundice and phototherapy for unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia or exchange transfusion prevents this potentially fatal consequence.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and F.</b></p><p>ABO isoimmunization (Choice A) occurs when maternal IgG antibodies to fetal red blood cell antigens cause either fetal or neonatal destruction of these red blood cells. Bilirubin production is increased, and an indirect hyperbilirubinemia ensues. This cause of neonatal jaundice will present with pathologic findings such as an early presentation and rapid rate of rise, neither of which are present in this case.</p><p>Biliary obstruction (Choice B) caused by biliary atresia is a common cause of direct hyperbilirubinemia. In congenital biliary atresia, the common bile duct is occluded or atretic and bile is unable to pass into the duodenum.</p><p>Dehydration (Choice C) is the mechanism behind breastfeeding jaundice. In the first week of life, as a breastfeeding mother's milk begins to establish, the infant may become dehydrated. Dehydration leads to increased enterohepatic circulation and reabsorption of bilirubin, resulting in indirect hyperbilirubinemia. In this case, the infant's consistent wet diapers and stools argue against a dehydrated state.</p><p>Erythrocyte enzyme defect (Choice D) such as glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency causes increased hemolysis of red blood cells and a subsequent increase in unconjugated bilirubin. This cause of neonatal jaundice would present with an early presentation and rapid rate of rise, along with a substantial decrease in the newborn's hematocrit.</p><p>Polycythemia (Choice F) in a neonate is defined as a hematocrit concentration greater than 65%. This infant's hematocrit is well below this cutoff. Neonates with polycythemia can develop hyperbilirubinemia as a result of increased turnover of excess red blood cells.</p>",
"44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cardiogenic shock can occur when decreased cardiac output leads to organ hypoperfusion, leading to hypotension, edematous extremities, cardiomegaly, decreased urine output, pulmonary edema, and respiratory distress. The intravenous administration of dopamine can be used in cases of cardiogenic shock or refractory hypotension, as it is a vasopressor and inotrope at high doses.</p><p>A patient with fever, hypotension, edematous extremities, cardiomegaly, decreased urine output, and respiratory distress raises suspicion for myocarditis and cardiogenic shock. Cardiogenic shock can occur when decreased cardiac output leads to organ hypoperfusion. In cardiogenic shock, dysfunction of cardiac myocytes results in decreased chronotropy, lusitropy, or inotropy. Decreased cardiac output and increased pulmonary capillary wedge pressure result from such dysfunction. Systemic vascular resistance is increased as a physiologic attempt to maintain mean arterial pressure in the setting of decreased cardiac output and peripheral perfusion. Physical examination findings can include tachycardia, tachypnea, crackles on auscultation of the lungs, and an S3 or S4 gallop on cardiac auscultation. Dopamine is a vasopressor and inotrope at increased doses. Intravenous administration of dopamine may be appropriate in cases of cardiogenic shock or refractory hypotension.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Intravenous administration of an isotonic fluid bolus (Choice A) is typically done initially in cases of distributive or hypovolemic shock. It would be harmful in a patient with cardiogenic shock because of impaired cardiac output and exacerbation of cardiogenic pulmonary edema, and it was already empirically attempted in this case.</p><p>Intravenous administration of furosemide (Choice C) would not be an initial step in the management of cardiogenic shock. Although total body fluid volume is increased (eg, pulmonary edema and extremity edema), cardiac output to the kidneys is decreased. Restoration of cardiac output and mean arterial pressure to prevent further damage to organs is the first priority.</p><p>Intravenous administration of nitroprusside (Choice D) would be detrimental to this patient who is hypotensive. Nitroprusside is an intravenous, titratable vasodilator that can be used for the treatment of hypertensive emergencies. It breaks down in circulation to release nitric oxide, which in turn activates guanylate cyclase in vascular smooth muscle to result in vascular smooth muscle relaxation and vasodilation.</p><p>Increased inhaled oxygen concentration (Choice E) would not be beneficial to this patient. Arterial blood gas analysis of this patient shows adequate partial pressure of oxygen.</p>",
"45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hereditary angioedema presents with recurrent episodes of swelling of the skin, face, lips, respiratory tract, and gastrointestinal tract. It is caused by a deficiency in C1 esterase inhibitor, which leads to the overproduction of bradykinin, a vasodilator.</p><p>Hereditary angioedema presents with intermittent episodes of swelling of the skin, face, and lips, as well as the mucosal lining of the respiratory and gastrointestinal system. It is caused by an autosomal dominant-inherited or de novo deficiency in C1 esterase inhibitor, which leads to overactivation of the complement system and overproduction of bradykinin. Bradykinin is a vasodilator that leads to the symptoms associated with hereditary angioedema. Episodes of angioedema can be caused by trauma, medications, and infections. They are not improved by antihistamines but commonly self-resolve. Further evaluation would also demonstrate a decreased C4 complement concentration in addition to the decreased concentration or activity of serum C1 esterase inhibitor.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Skin testing for environmental allergies (Choice A) may be useful in the evaluation of allergic reactions or anaphylaxis, which may also present with angioedema. However, anaphylaxis also causes wheezing as a result of laryngeal edema, urticaria, emesis, and hypotension. It is generally acute in onset following a specific exposure.</p><p>Measurement of serum angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) activity (Choice B) would not be useful in the evaluation of hereditary angioedema. However, decreased activity of ACE, as can be caused by ACE inhibitor medications, can lead to angioedema as a result of the decreased degradation of bradykinin. ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in hereditary angioedema.</p><p>Radioallergosorbent test (RAST) (Choice D) is a test used to identify antibodies against particular antigens, although it is no longer commonly used. Because hereditary angioedema is not caused by an allergic reaction, a RAST would not be useful in its evaluation beyond ruling out an allergic contributor which is unlikely on the basis of no specific exposure history.</p><p>Serum protein electrophoresis (Choice E) is commonly used to evaluate for malignancy (eg, multiple myeloma), as well as kidney and immunological disorders. It is not frequently used to evaluate the complement system and, thus, would have no role in diagnosing hereditary angioedema.</p>",
"46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The initial management of hypoxemia requires the administration of supplemental oxygen, regardless of cause. Bronchiolitis is a common lower respiratory tract infection in children less than 2 years of age, often secondary to infection with RSV. Treatment entails supplemental oxygen, frequent nasal suctioning, and consideration of an advanced airway if severe.</p><p>Bronchiolitis is a common cause of lower respiratory tract infection in children under the age of 2 years. It is typically caused by a viral infection, commonly from respiratory syncytial virus (RSV). It presents with a viral prodrome of fever, nasal congestion, and rhinorrhea followed by cough, dyspnea, and lethargy as inflammation progresses to the lower airway. Auscultation may disclose wheezes and crackles. Severe disease is marked by the presence of increased respiratory effort (tachypnea, accessory muscle use, intercostal retractions, nasal flaring, grunting), hypoxemia, and apnea. Patients presenting with severe disease should be hospitalized and immediately started on respiratory support with the administration of oxygen, nasal suctioning, and advanced airway and ventilatory support if respiratory failure is imminent.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, and H.</b></p><p>Arterial blood gas analysis (Choice A) may confirm the presence of hypoxemia but should not delay the administration of oxygen given the patient's decreased peripheral oxygen saturation.</p><p>Culture of the pharynx for bacteria (Choice B) is indicated for the workup of pharyngitis, which may present with fever, sore throat, and dysphonia. Causes include group A streptococcus, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, and viruses.</p><p>Viral culture of respiratory secretions (Choice C) may be included in the diagnostic workup to identify influenza, RSV, parainfluenza, adenovirus, or other causal agents. This information can aid decision-making for the use of pharmacologic therapies, isolation precautions, and prophylactic treatment of exposed contacts. Oxygen should be administered first.</p><p>X-rays of the chest (Choice D) and neck (Choice E) may be obtained to exclude alternative diagnoses for respiratory distress such as epiglottitis, bacterial pneumonia, pneumothorax, foreign body aspiration, or heart failure. The initial step in management is securing the airway and treating the patient's hypoxemia.</p><p>Administration of an inhaled bronchodilator (Choice F) may be considered in the emergency setting if there is ambiguity of the underlying diagnosis and concern for reactive airways disease. It does not particularly help with the treatment of bronchiolitis. As well, supplemental oxygen should be administered first in a patient with hypoxemia.</p><p>Subcutaneous administration of epinephrine (Choice H) is not a currently accepted therapy for bronchiolitis. Oxygen should be administered first. Subcutaneous epinephrine may be appropriate in the treatment of anaphylaxis or reactive airway disease.</p>",
"47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with abusive head trauma can present with encephalopathy, apnea, seizures, and retinal hemorrhages, and can have associated injuries such as rib fractures, long bone fractures, or skull fractures. The mechanism of abusive head trauma is typically inflicted by blunt force trauma or shaking, which results in an acceleration-deceleration force on the infant.</p><p>Severe intracranial injury with a history inconsistent with the severity of the patient's physical findings raises suspicion for abusive head trauma and nonaccidental trauma. Patients with abusive head trauma can present with encephalopathy, apnea, seizures, and retinal hemorrhages, and can have associated injuries such as rib fractures, long bone fractures, or skull fractures. Risk factors for nonaccidental trauma include unstable family situations, prior history of abuse, major birth defects, and parental depression. The mechanism of abusive head trauma is typically inflicted by blunt force trauma or shaking, which results in an acceleration-deceleration force on the infant. Infants are more vulnerable to deceleration injury because of several factors. The large head and weak neck musculature result in greater movement of the brain in the intracranial space when acted upon by acceleration and deceleration force such as in shaking. In addition, the infant skull is thin and flat, allowing forces to transfer through the skull to the brain. CT scan findings that are associated with nonaccidental head trauma include subdural hemorrhage (especially multiple hemorrhages or those in an interhemispheric or posterior fossa location), hypoxic-ischemic injury, and cerebral edema.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Apparent life-threatening event (Choice B) is a transient and unexpected change in an infant's breathing, appearance, or behavior. It is typically resolved by the time infants present for medical evaluation, and the history involves a brief episode of cyanosis or pallor, abnormal or absent breathing, marked change in tone, or altered level of consciousness.</p><p>Aspiration (Choice C) can present in infants as choking or coughing during feeding. Infants may also have apnea during feeding or failure to thrive resulting from insufficient intake and feeding difficulty. It would be unlikely to cause acute encephalopathy and intracranial injury.</p><p>Blunt trauma head injury (Choice D) can be severe enough to cause altered level of consciousness, behavioral changes, and vomiting. Infants with unintentional head trauma would present with a history consistent with head injury. Retinal hemorrhages, subdural hemorrhage, and cerebral edema are more significantly associated with abusive head trauma than with unintentional head injury.</p><p>Near drowning (Choice E) in infants can result in aspiration, hypoxemia, respiratory distress, and laryngospasm. Hypoxemia leads to ischemic neuronal damage resulting in cerebral edema. However, retinal hemorrhages and subdural hemorrhage are more likely to be secondary to abusive head trauma.</p><p>Suffocation (Choice F) can result in hypoxia, hypercarbia, loss of consciousness, and severe neurologic injury including seizures and coma. Other physical signs may include petechiae on the eyelids and bruising around the neck.</p>",
"48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: VSDs are common cardiac malformations that result in a left-to-right intracardiac shunt. They may form in isolation or in conjunction with other malformations. The majority are asymptomatic and will self-resolve, but severe disease can result in pulmonary hypertension and development of Eisenmenger syndrome if untreated.</p><p>Ventricular septal defects (VSD) are among the most common congenital heart malformations, and they may occur in isolation or in association with complex structural abnormalities. The genetic cause of VSDs is heterogeneous and includes known associations with well-defined syndromes such as Down, DiGeorge, or Holt-Oram syndrome. It may also occur in isolation and via nonspecific, random mutations and individual point mutations that are de novo. VSDs are characterized by a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border and may have an associated palpable thrill. VSDs result in a left-to-right shunt, which increases pulmonary blood flow. This leads to remodeling of the pulmonary vasculature and resultant smooth muscle hypertrophy. In turn, concentric narrowing of the pulmonary arterioles occurs, which results in the development of pulmonary arterial hypertension. If severe and progressive enough, this can cause increased right ventricular pressures and associated myocardial hypertrophy, which subsequently results in reversal of the shunt, presenting with shortness of breath and cyanosis. Increased pulmonary blood flow also results in increased blood return to the left atrium, which may cause left atrial enlargement.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Aortic stenosis (Choice A) presents with a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur, best heard at the upper right sternal border and radiates to the carotid arteries. It classically occurs secondary to age-related fibrotic and calcific changes of the valve but can occur earlier in life in cases of bicuspid aortic valve or rheumatic heart disease.</p><p>Atrial septal defect (Choice B) is a common congenital malformation of the interatrial septum resulting in a left-to-right shunt. Examination typically shows a fixed, widely split S2 caused by increased blood flow through the pulmonic valve. Severe defects can result in pulmonary hypertension and development of Eisenmenger syndrome over time, with reversal of the left-to-right shunt.</p><p>Pulmonary valve stenosis (Choice C) presents with a systolic murmur best heard in the second left intercostal region, which is generally crescendo-decrescendo in nature, though quieter and with less radiation to the lower neck than aortic stenosis because of lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation.</p><p>Tetralogy of Fallot (Choice D) consists of pulmonary infundibular stenosis, overriding aorta, VSD, and right ventricular hypertrophy, resulting in a right-to-left shunt with cyanosis first noted in infancy. The murmur heard with tetralogy of Fallot relates to the underlying VSD characterized by a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border. This otherwise healthy patient without other findings more likely has an isolated VSD.</p>",
"49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pertussis occurs as a result of an infection from Bordetella pertussis and may lead to severe paroxysmal coughing, which may be accompanied by cyanosis and followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization, and is treated with antibiotics, typically macrolides.</p><p>Episodes of severe paroxysmal coughing in the setting of a respiratory illness are concerning for pertussis. Pertussis occurs as a result of an infection from Bordetella pertussis and classically presents in three stages. The catarrhal stage is first, which presents with fever, rhinorrhea, and a mild cough. This progresses to the paroxysmal stage after 1 to 2 weeks, in which patients experience violent bouts of extreme coughing, at times violent enough to cause secondary traumatic injury such as rib fractures, urinary incontinence, and pneumothorax. Often, such coughing fits are accompanied by cyanosis and may be followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. The paroxysmal stage can last for weeks to months. Symptoms resolve during the convalescent stage, which lasts from 1 to 4 weeks. Pertussis toxin is a major virulence factor that inactivates the Gi subunits of G protein-coupled receptors in the respiratory epithelium and systemically, leading to uninhibited adenylyl cyclase activity and increased intracellular cAMP concentrations. In neutrophils and macrophages, this results in impaired recruitment, migration, and production of inflammatory mediators. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization in the form of acellular pertussis, typically administered along with tetanus and diphtheria vaccines (DTaP). It is treated with antibiotics, typically macrolides, and supportive care.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Aspiration caused by gastroesophageal reflux (Choice A) more commonly presents with symptoms in association with feeding and may also present with recurrent bacterial pneumonia and fever.</p><p>Chlamydial pneumonia (Choice B) may be preceded by neonatal conjunctivitis. It classically presents in infants with a staccato cough, in which each cough is separated by a brief inspiration. Physical examination may show tachypnea and crackles.</p><p>Congestive heart failure (Choice C) in an infant is likely to be preceded by signs and symptoms of cardiac malformations or dysfunction during the neonatal period. While congenital heart defects may lead to cyanosis, severe paroxysms of coughing are atypical and are more suggestive of pertussis.</p><p>Respiratory syncytial virus bronchiolitis (Choice E) is more likely to present with fever, tachypnea, rhinorrhea, and wheezing. Patients may also display apneic episodes, although severe paroxysms of coughing would be atypical.</p>",
"50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Malrotation occurs secondary to the incomplete rotation of bowel during embryonic development. This places the newborn at risk for midgut volvulus, which can present with bilious vomiting, abdominal distention, and pain, and when severe, intestinal necrosis, hematochezia, perforation, and peritonitis.</p><p>Midgut volvulus, a dangerous complication of intestinal malrotation, often occurs in children within the first few months of life. Malrotation results from the incomplete rotation of the midgut during embryonal development and leaves the midgut attached to a thin stalk of mesentery at the base of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) instead of the normally broad-based mesenteric stalk. The cecum also remains in an abnormal position within the mid-upper abdomen instead of the right lower quadrant and is attached to the lateral abdominal wall by fibrous bands which cross over the top of the duodenum and can also cause constriction. This leaves the small intestine at risk for volvulus at the base of the SMA. Volvulus can lead to bilious vomiting, abdominal distention, and vascular compromise of the small bowel with consequent intestinal necrosis, hematochezia, perforation, and peritonitis. The SMA is susceptible to constriction in midgut volvulus because of its anatomical proximity and embryonal relationship to the formation of the midgut. Evaluation of malrotation and midgut volvulus requires an upper gastrointestinal barium study, which will outline the patient's small bowel anatomy and further assess for the presence of an obstructive volvulus. The upper gastrointestinal barium study in this patient demonstrates a spiral appearance of the duodenum with an abnormal position of the duodenojejunal junction on the lateral view, which indicates the presence of malrotation with a midgut volvulus. Treatment requires emergent surgical correction.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Abnormal exocrine secretions from the pancreas and intestinal glands (Choice A) can occur in cystic fibrosis, which is caused by a failure of proper chloride channel function. This leads to inspissated stool and meconium ileus, which can present with abdominal distention but more commonly presents with absence of a bowel movement within the first 48 hours of life. It would not be expected to cause peritonitis, as in this patient.</p><p>Failure of cannulization of the proximal bowel (Choice B) results in duodenal atresia. It typically presents with signs of intestinal obstruction in the newborn, such as recurrent emesis and poor feeding. It is associated with Down syndrome, polyhydramnios in utero, and two adjacent contrast-filled structures on imaging. This patient was previously able to tolerate oral feeding, making this diagnosis less likely.</p><p>Failure of caudal migration of ganglion cells (Choice C) causes Hirschsprung disease, which classically presents with the failure to pass stool in the first 48 hours of life. Digital rectal examination commonly demonstrates an explosive release of stool, rather than gross blood, as in this patient.</p><p>Vascular occlusion in a segment of bowel (Choice E) can result in jejunal or ileal atresia. This most commonly occurs in the setting of maternal smoking or use of cocaine. The newborn typically presents with poor feeding and emesis, and on imaging exhibits multiple air-fluid levels in the small bowel proximal to the site of atresia causing obstruction.</p>"
};
